/* ---------- STEPS ---------- */
const STEPS = {};

STEPS.pressure = [
  { title: 'Atmosphere is the baseline',
    floor: `Stand outside and hold your hand flat. You cannot feel it, but there is a tall column of air sitting on your hand, pushing on every side of you. You are used to it, so it feels like nothing. That "nothing" is the starting line for almost every pressure number in the plant.`,
    words: `<span class="term" data-term="pressure">Pressure</span> is how hard something pushes on each square inch. The air around us, the <span class="term" data-term="atmosphere">atmosphere</span>, pushes at about 14.7 pounds per square inch (psi) at sea level. It is a little less up a mountain and it shifts a bit with the weather. Most plant gauges are built to read <strong>zero</strong> when they only feel the atmosphere.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 200" role="img" aria-label="Air column pushing on a gauge that reads zero"><rect x="40" y="20" width="120" height="120" fill="#1d3a55"/><text x="100" y="60" text-anchor="middle">air column</text><text x="100" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">about 14.7 psi</text><path class="ln" d="M100 95 V135"/><path d="M92 125 L100 140 L108 125" fill="#d6dde4"/><rect class="eq" x="30" y="140" width="140" height="14"/><circle class="bub" cx="360" cy="95" r="60"/><path class="amb" d="M360 95 L318 70"/><text x="360" y="130" text-anchor="middle" class="lb">0 psig</text><text x="360" y="178" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">A gauge open to air reads zero. The air did not go away.</text></svg>`,
    bite: `Nights, 0300. A gauge on a pump discharge reads 0 and a new hand says, "Good, it's empty, crack the flange." Zero only means "same as the air." The line was still full of liquid, and the gauge tap was plugged. Zero is a baseline, not a safety check.`,
    head: { q: 'A gauge open to the air reads 0. Is there really no pressure on it?', a: 'There is about 14.7 psi of air on it, but the gauge is built to call that zero. Zero on a gauge means "same as the atmosphere."' } },
  { title: 'psig vs psia',
    floor: `Think of a bathroom scale that you set to zero with nothing on it. If you set it to zero with a bag of groceries on it, every reading after that is "extra above the groceries." Pressure gauges do the same thing with the air.`,
    words: `<span class="term" data-term="psig">psig</span> means pounds per square inch <em>gauge</em>: pressure above the atmosphere. <span class="term" data-term="psia">psia</span> means pounds per square inch <em>absolute</em>: pressure above a perfect empty space. The math is simple: <strong>psia ≈ psig + 14.7</strong>. A tire at 32 psig is really about 46.7 psia. Most field gauges read psig. Vacuum and design work often use psia because it cannot go below zero.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>What you see</th><th>psig (gauge)</th><th>psia (absolute)</th></tr><tr><td>Gauge open to air</td><td>0</td><td>about 14.7</td></tr><tr><td>Pump discharge example</td><td>48</td><td>about 62.7</td></tr><tr><td>Tank under vacuum example</td><td>−5 (or "10 in Hg vac")</td><td>about 9.7</td></tr><tr><td>Perfect vacuum (you never get here)</td><td>about −14.7</td><td>0</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `The log sheet says "pressure 15." Day shift meant 15 psia on the vacuum column, which is almost exactly atmosphere. Night shift read it as 15 psig and thought the column was pressurized. Always write the unit with the letter: psig or psia.`,
    head: { q: 'A gauge reads 20 psig. About what is that in psia?', a: 'About 34.7 psia (20 + 14.7).' } },
  { title: 'Vacuum is "less than atmosphere"',
    floor: `Suck on a straw with your thumb over the end. Your cheeks pull in. You did not create a pulling force. You took some air out, and the outside air pushed in harder than the inside could push back. That is all vacuum is.`,
    words: `<span class="term" data-term="vacuum">Vacuum</span> is any pressure lower than the atmosphere around it. It is not "negative magic" and it does not suck. The outside air pushes. Plants measure vacuum a few ways: <span class="term" data-term="inches-hg">inches of mercury (in Hg)</span> for deeper vacuum, <span class="term" data-term="inches-wc">inches of water column (in WC)</span> for very small pressures like tank vents and draft, and psia or absolute units like torr for deep vacuum. About 29.9 in Hg of vacuum is the most you could ever pull at sea level. 1 psi is about 2 in Hg, and about 27.7 in WC.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 210" role="img" aria-label="Scale from atmosphere to full vacuum"><path class="ln" d="M60 40 H460"/><g class="sm"><text x="60" y="30" text-anchor="middle">0 in Hg vac</text><text x="200" y="30" text-anchor="middle">10 in Hg</text><text x="330" y="30" text-anchor="middle">20 in Hg</text><text x="460" y="30" text-anchor="middle">about 29.9</text></g><circle class="fillam" cx="60" cy="40" r="7"/><circle class="fillam" cx="200" cy="40" r="7"/><circle class="fillam" cx="330" cy="40" r="7"/><circle class="fillrd" cx="460" cy="40" r="7"/><text x="60" y="70" text-anchor="middle">14.7 psia</text><text x="200" y="70" text-anchor="middle">about 9.8 psia</text><text x="330" y="70" text-anchor="middle">about 4.9 psia</text><text x="460" y="70" text-anchor="middle">0 psia</text><text x="60" y="95" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">atmosphere</text><text x="460" y="95" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">perfect vacuum</text><text x="260" y="140" text-anchor="middle" class="am lb">More inches of vacuum = less air left inside.</text><text x="260" y="165" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">1 psi ≈ 2 in Hg ≈ 27.7 in WC (rounded for training)</text></svg>`,
    bite: `The board shows "vacuum 25" and someone says, "It went up to 25, so pressure went up." No. More inches of vacuum means <em>less</em> pressure inside. Say it as "deeper vacuum" or "less vacuum" so nobody gets turned around.`,
    head: { q: 'Vacuum went from 20 in Hg to 12 in Hg. Did the pressure inside go up or down?', a: 'Up. Less vacuum means more gas got in. Pressure inside moved closer to atmosphere.' } },
  { title: 'Why a tank or railcar can collapse',
    floor: `Take an empty plastic water bottle, hold it under hot water, cap it, then let it cool. It crumples by itself. Nothing grabbed it. The air inside shrank and the outside air pushed it flat.`,
    words: `Storage tanks are strong at holding liquid in, but many are weak at holding the atmosphere out. A big tank roof can have tons of air on it. If you pump liquid out, or steam-clean and then cool a closed tank, the inside pressure drops. If the vent or <span class="term" data-term="relief-valve">vacuum breaker</span> is blocked, painted over, frozen, or blinded, the outside air wins. Even a few <span class="term" data-term="inches-wc">inches of water column</span> of vacuum can buckle a large, thin-walled tank. Railcars and tank trucks can suck in the same way when they are steamed out and closed up.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 200" role="img" aria-label="Tank with a blocked vent being pumped out, walls pushed in"><rect class="eq" x="60" y="40" width="130" height="130" rx="8"/><rect class="liq" x="63" y="130" width="124" height="37"/><path class="red" d="M125 40 V18 M112 18 H138"/><text x="150" y="20" class="rt sm">vent blocked</text><path class="ln" d="M190 158 H250" /><text x="215" y="150" class="sm" text-anchor="middle">pump out</text><path class="eq" d="M320 40 Q350 80 320 105 Q352 140 320 170 H450 Q420 140 452 105 Q420 80 450 40 Z"/><path class="amb" d="M290 105 H320 M480 105 H452"/><text x="385" y="190" text-anchor="middle" class="am sm">outside air pushes the walls in</text></svg>`,
    bite: `Night shift finishes pumping a tank empty for cleaning. The flame arrester on the vent was plugged with product that froze. At 0500 there is a bang like a car wreck, and the tank shell is dented in. Before you pump down or cool a closed vessel, walk out and look at the vent path.`,
    head: { q: 'You are about to pump a tank empty. What do you confirm first to prevent collapse?', a: 'That the vent or vacuum breaker path is open and not blocked, frozen, or blinded.' } },
  { title: '"10 inches of vacuum on the suction"',
    floor: `Picture drinking a thick milkshake through a skinny straw. You pull hard and your cheeks cave in, but very little comes up. The harder you pull, the more you feel it. A pump feels the same on its suction side.`,
    words: `If a pump suction gauge shows <strong>10 in Hg vacuum</strong>, the pressure at the pump inlet is about 5 psi below atmosphere, roughly 9.8 psia. The pump is working to pull liquid in. Common causes: low tank level, a plugged <span class="term" data-term="strainer">strainer</span>, a partly closed suction valve, or thick or cold liquid. Low suction pressure moves you toward <span class="term" data-term="cavitation">cavitation</span>, where the liquid boils inside the pump. Many pumps normally run with a positive suction pressure, so a new vacuum reading is a clue to go look.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Suction reading</th><th>What it usually means</th><th>First look</th></tr><tr><td>+3 psig (normal for this example)</td><td>Tank head is feeding the pump</td><td>Nothing, log it</td></tr><tr><td>0 psig</td><td>Less feed pressure than normal</td><td>Tank level, suction valve</td></tr><tr><td>10 in Hg vacuum</td><td>Pump is starving and pulling hard</td><td>Strainer DP, tank level, valve lineup</td></tr><tr><td>20+ in Hg vacuum, gravel noise</td><td>Likely cavitating</td><td>Reduce rate per procedure, find the restriction</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A transfer pump normally shows +3 psig on suction. At 0200 it reads 10 in Hg vacuum and the discharge gauge is bouncing. Someone wants to open the discharge valve more to "get the rate back." That makes it worse. The problem is on the suction side: the basket strainer was packed with rags.`,
    head: { q: 'Suction went from +3 psig to 10 in Hg vacuum. Which side of the pump do you look at first?', a: 'The suction side: tank level, suction valve lineup, and the suction strainer.' } }
];

STEPS.dp = [
  { title: 'DP = two pressures talking',
    floor: `Put your thumb over the end of a garden hose. The pressure behind your thumb goes up and the water in front sprays out at lower pressure. Your thumb is making a difference between the two sides. You can feel that difference as the push on your thumb.`,
    words: `<span class="term" data-term="dp">Differential pressure (DP)</span> is the upstream pressure minus the downstream pressure. On drawings you will see ΔP ("delta P"), and the instrument that measures it is a <span class="term" data-term="pdt">PDT</span> (pressure differential transmitter) or a <span class="term" data-term="dp-cell">DP cell</span>. DP tells you how hard the fluid has to push to get through something: a filter, a valve, a meter, or a column of liquid.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 170" role="img" aria-label="Two pressure gauges on either side of a restriction"><path class="ln" d="M30 110 H210 M310 110 H490"/><rect class="eq" x="210" y="85" width="100" height="50" rx="4"/><text x="260" y="115" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">restriction</text><circle class="bub" cx="130" cy="55" r="26"/><path class="th" d="M130 81 V108"/><text x="130" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="lb">50</text><circle class="bub" cx="390" cy="55" r="26"/><path class="th" d="M390 81 V108"/><text x="390" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="lb">42</text><text x="260" y="160" text-anchor="middle" class="am lb">DP = 50 − 42 = 8 psi</text></svg>`,
    bite: `Two gauges on a filter read 50 and 42. The new operator writes "filter pressure 50" and moves on. The number that matters is the 8 psi difference. Last week it was 3. That filter is loading up, and nobody noticed for three shifts.`,
    head: { q: 'Upstream 60 psig, downstream 51 psig. What is the DP?', a: '9 psi.' } },
  { title: 'Dirty filter or strainer → DP up, flow down',
    floor: `Think of a vacuum cleaner bag. When it is new the vacuum pulls strong. As it fills, the motor sounds different and it barely picks up. The bag did not break. It got harder for air to get through.`,
    words: `When a <span class="term" data-term="strainer">strainer</span> or filter catches dirt, the open space gets smaller. The fluid has to push harder to get through, so <span class="term" data-term="filter-dp">filter DP</span> climbs. If the pump cannot push harder, flow drops. <strong>Filter DP up = more restriction.</strong> That is the opposite of a flow meter, where DP up usually means more flow. Keep those two straight.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Filter condition</th><th>DP (example)</th><th>Flow</th><th>Pump discharge pressure</th></tr><tr><td>Clean (just changed)</td><td>3 psi</td><td>normal</td><td>normal</td></tr><tr><td>Loading</td><td>8 psi</td><td>slightly down</td><td>slightly up</td></tr><tr><td>Near plugged</td><td>18 psi</td><td>well down</td><td>up</td></tr><tr><td>Element torn / bypassing</td><td>drops to 1 psi</td><td>back up</td><td>normal</td></tr></table></div><p class="fig-note">Example numbers only. Your site sets real change-out limits.</p>`,
    bite: `Filter DP climbs from 8 to 18, then suddenly falls to 1 and flow comes back. Someone logs "filter cleared itself." Filters do not clear themselves. The element likely tore and dirt is now heading for the downstream equipment. A sudden DP drop is a warning too.`,
    head: { q: 'Filter DP suddenly drops from 18 psi to 1 psi without a change-out. Good news?', a: 'No. Likely a torn element or an open bypass. Dirt may be getting through.' } },
  { title: 'Flow by DP (orifice): more flow, more DP',
    floor: `Blow through a drinking straw gently, then hard. The harder you blow, the more you feel the pressure build in your mouth. More flow through a small hole takes more push.`,
    words: `An <span class="term" data-term="orifice">orifice plate</span> is a flat plate with a hole, clamped in the pipe. As flow goes up, the DP across the plate goes up, and faster than the flow does. Double the flow gives about four times the DP. The transmitter does the square-root math and shows flow. <strong>Orifice DP up usually means more flow.</strong> The honest caveat: the meter assumes a certain density and a full pipe. If the fluid gets lighter or heavier, gets gas bubbles, or the impulse lines plug, the flow number will be wrong.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 180" role="img" aria-label="Orifice plate in a pipe with high and low pressure taps"><path class="ln" d="M20 100 H500"/><path class="eq" d="M258 70 V92 M258 108 V130" stroke-width="6"/><path class="th" d="M220 100 V50 H245 M300 100 V50 H275"/><circle class="bub" cx="260" cy="45" r="20"/><text x="260" y="50" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">FT</text><text x="200" y="45" class="sm" text-anchor="end">high side</text><text x="320" y="45" class="sm">low side</text><text x="260" y="160" text-anchor="middle" class="am lb">2× flow ≈ 4× DP</text></svg>`,
    bite: `A steam flow meter shows flow climbing all night while production is flat. The low-side impulse line was leaking, so the low-side pressure read low and the DP looked high. The meter "saw" more flow that was not there. When a flow number does not match what the rest of the unit is doing, suspect the meter.`,
    head: { q: 'On an orifice meter, DP goes up. What does that usually mean?', a: 'More flow, assuming the meter and the fluid are normal.' } },
  { title: 'Level by DP cell: wet and dry legs',
    floor: `Dive to the bottom of a swimming pool and your ears hurt more than near the top. The deeper the water above you, the more it pushes. A level transmitter feels the same thing.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="dp-cell">DP cell</span> on the bottom of a tank feels the weight of the liquid above it. More level means more pressure at the bottom. On a closed tank there is also gas pressure on top, so the cell's other side is piped to the top of the tank to cancel it out. If that top line is empty of liquid, it is a <strong>dry leg</strong>. If it is kept full of a known liquid on purpose, it is a <span class="term" data-term="wet-leg">wet leg</span>. The cell reads the difference, and the transmitter turns it into level. It is also tuned for one density, so a heavier or lighter liquid will read wrong.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 220" role="img" aria-label="Closed tank with DP cell, high side at bottom, low side leg to top"><rect class="eq" x="140" y="20" width="140" height="180" rx="10"/><rect class="liq" x="143" y="95" width="134" height="102"/><text x="210" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">gas / vapor</text><path class="th" d="M140 185 H90 V170"/><path class="th" d="M140 35 H60 V170"/><rect class="eq" x="50" y="170" width="50" height="30"/><text x="75" y="190" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">DP</text><text x="300" y="40" class="sm">low side: top of tank (dry or wet leg)</text><text x="300" y="185" class="sm">high side: bottom (liquid weight + gas)</text><text x="300" y="120" class="am lb">cell reads only the liquid part</text></svg>`,
    bite: `A wet-leg level on a hot tank slowly reads higher and higher all night. The fill liquid in the wet leg was boiling off, so the low side got lighter and the level looked high. Operators cut feed, and the tank really ran low. When a DP level trends oddly, check it against a sight glass or a second level.`,
    head: { q: 'A DP level cell is set up for water. The tank now holds a lighter liquid. Will it read high or low?', a: 'Low. Lighter liquid pushes less for the same height, so the cell sees less DP.' } },
  { title: 'Pump head ≈ discharge − suction',
    floor: `Stand at the bottom of a staircase holding a bucket. Carrying it up adds energy. A pump does the same to liquid: it takes it in at one pressure and sends it out at a higher one.`,
    words: `The pump's <span class="term" data-term="pump-head">developed pressure</span> is roughly discharge pressure minus suction pressure, when the two gauges are at about the same height and the lines are full of liquid. Example: <strong>suction 2 psig, discharge 48 psig → the pump is putting up about 46 psi.</strong> If that number drops at the same flow, the pump may be wearing, cavitating, air-bound, or spinning the wrong way. Engineers talk in feet of head, but for rounds, "psi across the pump" is a fine working number.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 170" role="img" aria-label="Pump with suction 2 psig and discharge 48 psig"><path class="ln" d="M30 110 H200 M290 110 H490"/><circle class="eq" cx="245" cy="110" r="40"/><path class="th" d="M225 90 L275 110 L225 130 z"/><circle class="bub" cx="130" cy="55" r="26"/><path class="th" d="M130 81 V108"/><text x="130" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="lb">2</text><text x="130" y="20" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">suction psig</text><circle class="bub" cx="380" cy="55" r="26"/><path class="th" d="M380 81 V108"/><text x="380" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="lb">48</text><text x="380" y="20" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">discharge psig</text><text x="260" y="165" text-anchor="middle" class="am lb">48 − 2 ≈ 46 psi put up by the pump</text></svg>`,
    bite: `After a motor was rewired, a pump showed 2 psig suction and only 20 psig discharge instead of 48. Flow was low but "something was coming out." It was spinning backward. A centrifugal pump running backward still pumps a little, which fools people. Check rotation after electrical work.`,
    head: { q: 'Suction 5 psig, discharge 65 psig. About how much is the pump putting up?', a: 'About 60 psi.' } }
];

STEPS.pid = [
  { title: 'P&ID vs PFD vs isometric',
    floor: `Think about directions to a friend's house. One version says "take the highway to town." Another shows every street, stop sign, and turn. A third shows exactly how the driveway bends. Plant drawings come in the same three flavors.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="pfd">PFD</span> (process flow diagram) is the big picture: main equipment and main flows, often with design rates. A <span class="term" data-term="pid">P&amp;ID</span> (piping and instrumentation diagram) shows every line, valve, instrument, and control loop. It is the operator's map. An <span class="term" data-term="isometric">isometric</span> ("iso") is a 3-D-looking pipe drawing that shows real routing, lengths, and fittings for the pipe fitters. None of them are drawn to scale in the field. A P&amp;ID is about how things connect, not where they sit.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Drawing</th><th>Shows</th><th>Operator uses it for</th></tr><tr><td>PFD</td><td>Main equipment, main flow paths</td><td>Understanding what the unit does</td></tr><tr><td>P&amp;ID</td><td>Every line, valve, instrument, loop, tag</td><td>Line-ups, isolation, troubleshooting, LOTO support</td></tr><tr><td>Isometric</td><td>Real pipe routing, elevations, fittings</td><td>Finding a line in a pipe rack, maintenance jobs</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `An operator used the PFD to plan an isolation. The PFD did not show the small bypass around the control valve. Product kept flowing through the bypass. For isolation, use the current P&amp;ID and walk it.`,
    head: { q: 'Which drawing do you use to find every valve for an isolation?', a: 'The current P&ID, confirmed by walking the line.' } },
  { title: 'Lines, arrows, and reducers',
    floor: `A road map uses thick lines for highways, thin lines for side roads, and dotted lines for trails. A P&amp;ID uses line styles the same way, so you can tell pipe from wire from air signal at a glance.`,
    words: `A thick solid line is usually a main process pipe. Thinner solid lines are smaller or utility lines. Dashed lines are usually electrical signals. Lines with small slashes or circles are often pneumatic (air) or data links. Your drawing's legend sheet is the final word. Arrows show normal flow direction. A <span class="term" data-term="reducer">reducer</span> looks like a small trapezoid and means the pipe changes size. Line labels often carry size, service code, line number, and material spec.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 210" role="img" aria-label="Common line types"><path class="ln" d="M30 30 H250" marker-end=""/><text x="270" y="35">main process line</text><path class="th" d="M30 70 H250"/><text x="270" y="75">smaller / utility line</text><path class="sig" d="M30 110 H250"/><text x="270" y="115">electrical or instrument signal</text><path class="th" d="M30 150 H250"/><g class="th"><path d="M70 143 L80 157 M76 143 L86 157 M150 143 L160 157 M156 143 L166 157"/></g><text x="270" y="155">pneumatic (air) signal, many sites</text><path class="ln" d="M30 190 H110"/><path class="eq" d="M110 180 L140 185 L140 195 L110 200 z"/><path class="th" d="M140 190 H250"/><text x="270" y="195">reducer: pipe gets smaller</text></svg><p class="fig-note">Line styles vary by company. Always check your legend sheet.</p>`,
    bite: `A contractor traced a dashed line thinking it was a small drain. It was a signal line to a shutdown valve. Nobody got hurt, but the drawing was misread because nobody opened the legend sheet.`,
    head: { q: 'Where do you find what a line style means on your plant drawings?', a: 'The legend (symbol) sheet for that drawing set.' } },
  { title: 'Equipment symbols',
    floor: `Kids' drawings of a house are a square with a triangle. Nobody mistakes it for a car. Equipment symbols work the same way. They are simple outlines that everyone agrees on.`,
    words: `Common shapes: a <span class="term" data-term="tank">tank or vessel</span> is a tall rounded rectangle. A <span class="term" data-term="centrifugal-pump">centrifugal pump</span> is a circle with a discharge nozzle or triangle. A filter or <span class="term" data-term="strainer">strainer</span> is a box with a dashed or diagonal line (the screen). A <span class="term" data-term="heat-exchanger">heat exchanger</span> is a circle or long shell with a zig-zag, or two lines crossing. A <span class="term" data-term="ejector">steam ejector</span> is a narrow cone shape, and a <span class="term" data-term="liquid-ring">vacuum pump</span> is a circle with an inner circle. Every piece has a tag like T-101, P-101, F-101, E-101.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 560 200" role="img" aria-label="Equipment symbols"><rect class="eq" x="20" y="30" width="60" height="100" rx="10"/><text x="50" y="160" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">tank T-101</text><circle class="eq" cx="140" cy="90" r="30"/><path class="th" d="M125 75 L160 90 L125 105 z"/><text x="140" y="160" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">pump P-101</text><rect class="eq" x="200" y="60" width="60" height="60"/><path class="th" d="M200 120 L260 60" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/><text x="230" y="160" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">filter F-101</text><circle class="eq" cx="330" cy="90" r="32"/><path class="th" d="M300 90 L312 75 L324 105 L336 75 L348 105 L360 90"/><text x="330" y="160" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">exchanger E-101</text><path class="eq" d="M400 80 H430 L470 88 L510 80 V100 L470 92 L430 100 H400 z"/><text x="455" y="160" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">steam ejector</text><circle class="eq" cx="455" cy="40" r="0"/></svg>`,
    bite: `A new operator saw two circles close together on the drawing and assumed both were pumps. One was a pump. The other was a small exchanger. Read the tag letters under the symbol, not just the shape.`,
    head: { q: 'What does the letter in a tag like E-101 or P-101 tell you?', a: 'The kind of equipment: E for exchanger, P for pump, T for tank, F for filter (letters vary by site).' } },
  { title: 'Valve symbols',
    floor: `Picture a bow tie. Two triangles point at each other. That is the basic valve symbol. The small marks added to it tell you what kind of valve it is.`,
    words: `A plain bow tie is usually a <span class="term" data-term="gate-valve">gate valve</span>. A bow tie with a filled dot in the middle is a <span class="term" data-term="globe-valve">globe valve</span>. A bow tie with a circle is a <span class="term" data-term="ball-valve">ball valve</span>. A line or disk drawn across the middle is a <span class="term" data-term="butterfly-valve">butterfly valve</span>. A bow tie with a filled half and an arrow, or a Z-line, is a <span class="term" data-term="check-valve">check valve</span>, and it only lets flow go one way. A valve with a dome or box on top (the actuator) is a <span class="term" data-term="control-valve">control valve</span>, moved by a signal instead of a hand.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 600 170" role="img" aria-label="Valve symbols"><g><path class="eq" d="M20 70 L60 100 L60 70 L20 100 z"/><text x="40" y="135" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">gate</text></g><g><path class="eq" d="M110 70 L150 100 L150 70 L110 100 z"/><circle class="fillam" cx="130" cy="85" r="6"/><text x="130" y="135" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">globe</text></g><g><path class="eq" d="M200 70 L240 100 L240 70 L200 100 z"/><circle class="bub" cx="220" cy="85" r="9"/><text x="220" y="135" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">ball</text></g><g><path class="eq" d="M290 70 L330 100 L330 70 L290 100 z"/><path class="amb" d="M310 64 V106"/><text x="310" y="135" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">butterfly</text></g><g><path class="eq" d="M380 70 L420 100 L420 70 L380 100 z"/><path class="fillam" d="M400 85 L420 70 L420 100 z"/><text x="400" y="135" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">check (flow →)</text></g><g><path class="eq" d="M490 80 L530 110 L530 80 L490 110 z"/><path class="th" d="M510 95 V60"/><path class="eq" d="M490 60 A20 20 0 0 1 530 60 z"/><text x="510" y="135" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">control (FV)</text></g></svg><p class="fig-note">Symbol details vary by company. Your legend sheet wins.</p>`,
    bite: `On a new unit, the drawing showed a check valve in a pump discharge, but in the field the check had been removed during a repair and never put back. When the pump tripped, liquid ran backward and spun the pump the wrong way. Drawings show intent. The field shows the truth.`,
    head: { q: 'On a drawing, a valve has a dome on top connected to a dashed line. What is it?', a: 'A control valve with an actuator, moved by a signal from a controller.' } },
  { title: 'ISA-style tags: reading the letters',
    floor: `License plates follow a pattern, so you can tell a state from the letters. Instrument tags follow a pattern too. Once you learn it, a tag reads like a short sentence.`,
    words: `Most plants use an <span class="term" data-term="isa-tag">ISA-style tag</span>. The <strong>first letter is what is being measured</strong>: F = flow, P = pressure, T = temperature, L = level, A = analysis, V = vibration. The <strong>next letters say what the device does</strong>: I = indicate, R = record, C = control, T = transmit, A = alarm, V = valve. The number ties the loop together. So <span class="term" data-term="fic">FIC-101</span> is a flow indicating controller, <span class="term" data-term="pit">PIT-101</span> is a pressure indicating transmitter, and FV-101 is the flow valve that FIC-101 moves. <span class="term" data-term="pdt">PDT-101</span> is a pressure differential transmitter.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Tag</th><th>Read it as</th></tr><tr><td><span class="tag">TI-101</span></td><td>Temperature Indicator</td></tr><tr><td><span class="tag">TIC-101</span></td><td>Temperature Indicating Controller</td></tr><tr><td><span class="tag">PI-101</span></td><td>Pressure Indicator (often a local gauge)</td></tr><tr><td><span class="tag">PIC-101</span></td><td>Pressure Indicating Controller</td></tr><tr><td><span class="tag">FI-101</span> / <span class="tag">FIC-101</span></td><td>Flow Indicator / Flow Indicating Controller</td></tr><tr><td><span class="tag">FV-101</span></td><td>Flow Valve, the control valve in the flow loop</td></tr><tr><td><span class="tag">PDT-101</span></td><td>Pressure Differential Transmitter</td></tr><tr><td><span class="tag">LIC-101</span></td><td>Level Indicating Controller</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `On nights the board asked for a check of "PI-101." The operator went to PIT-101, a transmitter in a different spot, and reported the wrong number. The tags look alike. Read every letter, and match the tag plate in the field.`,
    head: { q: 'Read this tag: LIC-101.', a: 'Level Indicating Controller, loop 101.' } },
  { title: 'Worked loop: click every symbol',
    floor: `Put your finger on the feed tank and trace the pipe to the right, saying each thing out loud. That is exactly what you do on a real drawing before a line-up. Slow is fast here.`,
    words: `Liquid leaves tank T-101 and goes into pump P-101. <span class="term" data-term="pit">PIT-101</span> reads suction pressure and PIT-102 reads discharge pressure. The pump pushes through filter F-101, and <span class="term" data-term="pdt">PDT-101</span> watches the DP across it. An <span class="term" data-term="orifice">orifice</span> (FT-101) measures flow and sends it to <span class="term" data-term="fic">FIC-101</span>. FIC-101 compares flow to its setpoint and moves <span class="term" data-term="control-valve">FV-101</span> to hold it. Flow ends in tank T-102. <strong>Click any symbol below</strong> and Plant Mentor will explain it.`,
    pic: LOOP_SVG + `<p class="fig-note">Tap a symbol to open Plant Mentor for it.</p>`,
    bite: `Flow on FIC-101 is low and FV-101 is 100% open. Someone wants to call instruments about the valve. Trace the loop first: PDT-101 is high, PIT-102 is up. The filter is plugging. The valve is wide open because it is trying to make up for the filter.`,
    head: { q: 'In this loop, which instrument tells you the filter is loading up?', a: 'PDT-101, the DP across filter F-101.' } }
];

STEPS.readings = [
  { title: 'Flow: what the board number means',
    floor: `Fill a bucket from a hose. You can tell how fast it fills by watching and by the sound. The board flow number is the plant's version of watching the bucket fill, but it comes from one meter in one spot.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="flow-reading">flow reading</span> tells you how much is moving past the meter, in units like gallons per minute (gpm) or pounds per hour. It is usually shown by an FI or <span class="term" data-term="fic">FIC</span> on the <span class="term" data-term="dcs">DCS</span> (distributed control system, the board). The meter might be an <span class="term" data-term="orifice">orifice</span>, a magnetic meter, a mass meter, or another type. Each has weak spots: air bubbles, a partly empty pipe, the wrong density, or a plugged tap. Flow should agree with other things: tank levels changing, pump amps, and valve position.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Flow says</th><th>Confirm with</th></tr><tr><td>Normal flow</td><td>Source tank level falling at the right rate, destination rising</td></tr><tr><td>Flow dropped</td><td>Pump amps down? Filter DP up? Valve position changed?</td></tr><tr><td>Flow at zero</td><td>Is the pump running? Is it really zero, or did the transmitter fail low?</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `FI-101 shows a steady 120 gpm all shift. At turnover, the receiving tank has barely moved. The meter had frozen at its last value when its signal failed. A perfectly flat line on a live process is a red flag.`,
    head: { q: 'Flow reads normal but the receiving tank level is not rising. What do you suspect?', a: 'The flow reading is wrong, or flow is going somewhere else (a wrong line-up or a leak). Check both.' } },
  { title: 'Level: what the board number means',
    floor: `Look at a coffee maker's water window. You see the water line through the glass. A <span class="term" data-term="sight-glass">sight glass</span> on a tank does the same thing, and the board level is supposed to match it.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="level-reading">level reading</span> is usually shown as percent of a set range, not feet or gallons. 0% might not be empty, and 100% might not be full. It is just the range the transmitter was set to. Level can come from a <span class="term" data-term="dp-cell">DP cell</span>, radar, float, or other type. Foam, a density change, a plugged tap, or a boiling wet leg can all fool it. <span class="term" data-term="lic">LIC</span> is a level controller that moves a valve to hold level.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 200" role="img" aria-label="Tank showing 0 percent is above the bottom and 100 percent below the top"><rect class="eq" x="160" y="20" width="140" height="170" rx="10"/><rect class="liq" x="163" y="100" width="134" height="87"/><path class="amb" d="M150 45 H310 M150 160 H310"/><text x="320" y="50" class="am">100% (top of range)</text><text x="320" y="165" class="am">0% (bottom of range)</text><text x="320" y="185" class="sm">liquid can still be below 0%</text><text x="320" y="30" class="sm">space above 100%</text></svg>`,
    bite: `The board showed a tank at 5% and the operator figured it was nearly empty, so he started a transfer in. The tank still had several feet of liquid below the 0% tap, plus the transfer. It overflowed at the top. Know what 0% and 100% mean on each tank.`,
    head: { q: 'Does 0% level always mean the tank is empty?', a: 'No. It means the bottom of the transmitter range. There may still be liquid below it.' } },
  { title: 'Temperature: what the board number means',
    floor: `Put a meat thermometer in a roast. It tells you the temperature at the tip, not the whole roast. Plant temperature readings work the same way. They tell you about one spot.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="temp-reading">temperature reading</span> (TI) usually comes from a sensor sitting in a <span class="term" data-term="thermowell">thermowell</span>, a metal pocket that sticks into the pipe. The reading lags the real process a little, because heat has to soak through the metal. <span class="term" data-term="tic">TIC</span> is a temperature controller, often moving steam or cooling water. If there is no flow past the thermowell, it reads the temperature of the pipe, not the process.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 170" role="img" aria-label="Thermowell sticking into a pipe"><path class="ln" d="M20 110 H500"/><path class="ln" d="M20 150 H500"/><rect class="eq" x="245" y="60" width="30" height="20"/><rect class="eq" x="252" y="80" width="16" height="50" rx="8"/><text x="290" y="72">TI-101 in a thermowell</text><path class="amb" d="M60 130 H200" marker-end=""/><text x="60" y="165" class="sm">no flow = reading drifts toward pipe/ambient temperature</text></svg>`,
    bite: `A heater outlet TI read a perfect temperature. Flow through the heater had nearly stopped, so the thermowell was sitting in stagnant liquid. The tubes were overheating where there was no sensor. Temperature only means something if the fluid is moving past it.`,
    head: { q: 'Why can a temperature reading lie when there is no flow?', a: 'The sensor reads the still liquid or pipe near it, not the process that is supposed to be passing by.' } },
  { title: 'A good temperature with a bad flow is still trouble',
    floor: `If your car's temperature gauge looks fine but the coolant has leaked out, the gauge might read normal right up until the engine seizes. One good number does not mean the system is healthy.`,
    words: `Readings are a team. Temperature needs flow to be meaningful. Level needs flow in and out to make sense. <span class="term" data-term="pressure">Pressure</span> needs to match pump amps and valve position. When one number looks good but its partner looks bad, believe the bad one until you prove otherwise. On the board, a good habit is to scan pairs: <strong>flow + temperature, level + flow, pressure + amps</strong>.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Looks good</th><th>Looks bad</th><th>What it may really be</th></tr><tr><td>Exchanger outlet temperature normal</td><td>Flow very low</td><td>Thermowell in dead liquid, or overheating elsewhere</td></tr><tr><td>Tank level steady</td><td>Inflow and outflow both zero</td><td>Stuck transmitter, or nothing moving</td></tr><tr><td>Discharge pressure normal</td><td>Amps way low</td><td>Pump dead-headed or losing prime</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `Night shift saw a cooling water return temperature "right on." The cooling water flow meter had failed low hours earlier and nobody paired it. The exchanger was barely getting any water, and the product went out hot to storage. Always look at the partner number.`,
    head: { q: 'Temperature looks normal but flow has dropped a lot. What do you trust?', a: 'Treat the low flow as real until proven otherwise. Go look.' } }
];

STEPS.pumps = [
  { title: 'Centrifugal vs positive displacement',
    floor: `A box fan pushes air by spinning. If you block the front, it just churns. A bicycle pump pushes a fixed amount each stroke. If you block the hose, the pressure builds until something gives. Plant pumps come in those same two families.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="centrifugal-pump">centrifugal pump</span> spins an impeller and flings liquid outward. Flow goes down as discharge pressure goes up, and if you close the discharge, pressure only rises to a limit. A <span class="term" data-term="pd-pump">positive displacement (PD) pump</span> such as a gear, lobe, piston, screw, or diaphragm pump moves a fixed amount per turn or stroke. Block its discharge and pressure keeps climbing until a relief lifts or something breaks. That is why PD pumps need a <span class="term" data-term="relief-valve">relief valve</span> and must never start against a closed valve.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th></th><th>Centrifugal</th><th>Positive displacement</th></tr><tr><td>Feels like</td><td>A fan</td><td>A bicycle pump</td></tr><tr><td>Closed discharge</td><td>Pressure tops out, pump heats up</td><td>Pressure climbs until relief lifts or something breaks</td></tr><tr><td>Flow control</td><td>Throttle discharge or change speed</td><td>Change speed or recycle. Never throttle shut.</td></tr><tr><td>Common use</td><td>Water, thin liquids, big flows</td><td>Thick liquids, slurry, metering, high pressure</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A new hand treated a gear pump like the centrifugals and started it with the discharge closed "to build pressure first." The relief was set right and lifted, but the relief had been piped to a drain that was plugged. Hose and gasket failures followed. Know which kind of pump you are standing next to.`,
    head: { q: 'Which pump type must never be started against a closed discharge?', a: 'A positive displacement pump.' } },
  { title: 'Suction, discharge, prime, dead-head',
    floor: `Try to drink from a straw that is only half in the glass. You get air and slurping, not a drink. A pump has the same problem if its suction side is not full of liquid.`,
    words: `Suction is the inlet side and discharge is the outlet side. A centrifugal pump needs its casing full of liquid before it can pump. That is called <span class="term" data-term="prime">prime</span>. If it runs full of air or vapor it is "air-bound" and moves almost nothing. To <span class="term" data-term="dead-head">dead-head</span> a pump means to run it with no flow out, such as a closed discharge valve or a blocked line. The energy turns into heat, and the liquid inside can boil and damage the seal and impeller. Many pumps have a <span class="term" data-term="min-flow">minimum flow line</span> back to the tank to prevent this.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 190" role="img" aria-label="Pump with suction from tank, discharge closed, and min flow recycle line"><rect class="eq" x="20" y="40" width="70" height="120" rx="9"/><rect class="liq" x="23" y="90" width="64" height="67"/><path class="ln" d="M90 150 H200"/><circle class="eq" cx="230" cy="150" r="28"/><path class="ln" d="M258 150 H420"/><path class="red" d="M380 135 V165"/><text x="400" y="130" class="rt sm">discharge closed</text><path class="th" d="M320 150 V60 H55 V40" stroke-dasharray="6 3"/><text x="190" y="52" class="sm">minimum-flow line back to tank</text><text x="140" y="180" class="sm">suction</text><text x="300" y="180" class="sm">discharge</text></svg>`,
    bite: `A pump at a tank farm was started with the discharge valve closed "just for a minute" while the operator went to open the far end. A radio call pulled him away for twenty minutes. The seal failed from heat and the pump sprayed product. Never leave a dead-headed pump.`,
    head: { q: 'What is "dead-heading" a pump?', a: 'Running it with no flow out, like against a closed discharge. The energy turns into heat.' } },
  { title: 'Cavitation: the gravel sound',
    floor: `Put your thumb over a garden hose and the water speeds up and sprays. Now picture the opposite on the pump's suction side: the water pressure drops so low that tiny bubbles form, then collapse hard when they hit the high-pressure side. It sounds like the pump is full of gravel.`,
    words: `<span class="term" data-term="cavitation">Cavitation</span> happens when suction pressure falls so low that the liquid boils right at the impeller eye, even at normal temperature. Every liquid has a <span class="term" data-term="vapor-pressure">vapor pressure</span>, the pressure where it boils at that temperature. Warmer liquid boils easier. <span class="term" data-term="npsh">NPSH</span> (net positive suction head) is the plain question: "Is there enough pressure at the pump inlet, above the boiling point, to keep this liquid a liquid?" Signs: gravel noise, jumpy discharge pressure, jumpy amps, flow swings, and vibration. Causes: low tank level, a plugged <span class="term" data-term="strainer">strainer</span>, a pinched suction valve, hot liquid, or too much flow.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>You notice</th><th>Likely suction-side cause</th></tr><tr><td>Gravel/marbles noise, vibration</td><td>Low suction pressure → bubbles collapsing</td></tr><tr><td>Discharge gauge bouncing</td><td>Pump losing grip on the liquid</td></tr><tr><td>Suction reads vacuum when it is normally positive</td><td>Low level, plugged strainer, suction valve pinched</td></tr><tr><td>Only happens on hot days or hot product</td><td>Liquid closer to boiling. Less margin.</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A pump started "rattling" every night around 0200. Days said it was fine. At night, the tank got pumped down lower and the product ran warmer from the reactor. Low level plus warm liquid meant not enough suction margin. The fix was a level limit, not a new pump.`,
    head: { q: 'A pump sounds like it is full of gravel and the discharge gauge is bouncing. What side do you check first?', a: 'The suction side: level, suction valve, strainer, and liquid temperature.' } },
  { title: 'Seals vs packing: field-check level',
    floor: `Think of a faucet handle. Where the stem comes out of the faucet body there is a small seal to keep water from dribbling out. A pump shaft has the same problem, but it is spinning fast.`,
    words: `<span class="term" data-term="packing">Packing</span> is soft rope-like rings squeezed around the shaft. It is supposed to drip a little to stay cool and lubricated, a few drops a minute. A mechanical <span class="term" data-term="seal">seal</span> uses two very flat faces rubbing together, one spinning and one still. It should not visibly leak. Many seals have a seal pot or flush line that keeps clean liquid or barrier fluid on the faces. At field-check level: packing drips a little, seals stay dry, and seal pots keep a steady level and pressure.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Check</th><th>Packing</th><th>Mechanical seal</th></tr><tr><td>Normal</td><td>Slow drip, gland cool enough</td><td>Dry, maybe a little dampness</td></tr><tr><td>Problem</td><td>Stream of liquid, or no drip and smoking hot</td><td>Steady drip or spray, seal pot level dropping or rising</td></tr><tr><td>Operator move</td><td>Report. Adjust only if trained.</td><td>Report. Check flush/seal pot. Plan a swap.</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `An operator saw a packed pump with no drip at all and thought, "Great, no leak." The packing had been over-tightened, it was running dry, and it scored the shaft sleeve. A little drip on packing is healthy. A dry, smoking gland is not.`,
    head: { q: 'Should packing drip a little?', a: 'Yes, a slow drip keeps it cool and lubricated. A mechanical seal should not visibly leak.' } },
  { title: 'Pump rounds: what to check every time',
    floor: `When you walk up to your truck, you glance at the tires, look for drips, and listen when it starts. Pump rounds are that same habit, done every shift, so you catch the change before it becomes a failure.`,
    words: `Walk up and use your senses: noise (new rattles, squeals, gravel), heat (bearings and seal area, check with the back of your hand near it, or a temperature gun, never grab), leaks (seal, flanges, drain plugs), <span class="term" data-term="amps">amps</span> on the motor, and suction and discharge pressure. Check the seal pot or flush. Compare to your normal numbers. Write down anything that changed, even small.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Round item</th><th>Normal (example)</th><th>Report if…</th></tr><tr><td>Noise</td><td>Steady hum</td><td>Gravel, squeal, knocking</td></tr><tr><td>Heat</td><td>Warm bearings</td><td>Too hot to hold your hand near, smoke, discolored paint</td></tr><tr><td>Leak</td><td>Dry seal / slow drip packing</td><td>Steady drip, spray, puddle</td></tr><tr><td>Amps</td><td>About the same as last shift</td><td>Big change up or down</td></tr><tr><td>Suction / discharge</td><td>2 psig / 48 psig</td><td>Suction lower, discharge jumpy, difference shrinking</td></tr><tr><td>Seal pot</td><td>Level and pressure steady</td><td>Level rising (process leaking in) or falling (barrier leaking out)</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `Amps on a pump dropped from 42 to 30 over two nights. Nobody wrote it down because "it was running." The coupling was slipping and the impeller was eroding. A number that changes slowly is still a change.`,
    head: { q: 'Amps on a pump dropped a lot at the same valve position. Name one thing that could explain it.', a: 'Less flow: a restriction, losing prime, a closed valve, a worn impeller, or a coupling issue.' } }
];

STEPS.valves = [
  { title: 'Field ID vs the drawing',
    floor: `You get sent to "the second valve on the left" and there are four valves. Which one? That is why valves carry tags. Guessing is how the wrong line gets opened.`,
    words: `On the <span class="term" data-term="pid">P&amp;ID</span>, each important valve has a symbol and often a tag number. In the field, look for a metal or plastic tag on the handwheel or body. Match three things: the tag, the line it sits on, and the direction of flow. Small manual valves may not be tagged, so find them by position relative to tagged equipment. When the field does not match the drawing, stop and report it.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Check</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Tag on the valve matches the drawing</td><td>Right valve</td></tr><tr><td>It sits on the line you traced</td><td>Tags can be moved or missing</td></tr><tr><td>Flow arrow or check direction matches</td><td>Checks and some valves only work one way</td></tr><tr><td>Anything added since the last drawing revision?</td><td>New bypasses and drains get missed</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `During a night line-up, two identical valves sat side by side on a pump header. The tag had fallen off one. The operator opened the wrong one and sent product to the wrong tank for an hour. When a tag is missing, trace the pipe, and get it re-tagged.`,
    head: { q: 'A valve tag is missing. How do you confirm it is the right valve?', a: 'Trace the pipe from tagged equipment and compare to the P&ID. Then report the missing tag.' } },
  { title: 'Isolation vs throttle vs check vs control',
    floor: `A house has a main water shutoff (all or nothing), a faucet handle (in between), and a backflow preventer (one way only). Plant valves have the same jobs, and each type is good at some jobs and bad at others.`,
    words: `<span class="term" data-term="isolation">Isolation</span> valves are fully open or fully closed: <span class="term" data-term="gate-valve">gate</span>, <span class="term" data-term="ball-valve">ball</span>, and many plug valves. <span class="term" data-term="throttle">Throttling</span> valves hold an in-between position to control flow: <span class="term" data-term="globe-valve">globe</span> and some <span class="term" data-term="butterfly-valve">butterfly</span> valves. <span class="term" data-term="check-valve">Check valves</span> let flow go one way only and are worked by the flow itself. <span class="term" data-term="control-valve">Control valves</span> are throttling valves with an actuator, moved automatically by a controller.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Job</th><th>Good choice</th><th>Poor choice</th></tr><tr><td>Isolate for maintenance</td><td>Gate, ball (per procedure)</td><td>Globe alone, control valve, check valve</td></tr><tr><td>Hand-throttle flow</td><td>Globe</td><td>Gate half-open</td></tr><tr><td>Stop backflow</td><td>Check</td><td>Anything that needs a person to close it</td></tr><tr><td>Automatic flow control</td><td>Control valve (FV)</td><td>Manual gate</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `Maintenance was told the line was isolated because the control valve was closed at 0%. Control valves are not tight shutoff valves, and the signal can change. Product leaked through while the flange was open. Isolation means the right block valves, locked, per procedure.`,
    head: { q: 'Is a control valve at 0% an isolation?', a: 'No. It can pass and it can move. Isolate with block valves per your LOTO procedure.' } },
  { title: 'Fail open, fail close, fail last',
    floor: `A screen door with a spring closes by itself when you let go. Some doors are propped to stay open. Control valves are set up the same way: when they lose air or signal, a spring drives them to a chosen position.`,
    words: `Every control valve has a <span class="term" data-term="fail-position">fail position</span>, the position it goes to if it loses instrument air or signal. <strong>Fail close (FC)</strong>: spring shuts it, used where flow stopping is the safe way, like feed into a tank. <strong>Fail open (FO)</strong>: spring opens it, used where flow is the safe way, like cooling water to an exchanger. <strong>Fail last (FL)</strong>: stays where it was, used on some special services. The drawing often marks FC, FO, or FL next to the valve.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Valve (example)</th><th>Fail position</th><th>Why that is the safe way</th></tr><tr><td>FV-101 feed to tank T-102</td><td>Fail close</td><td>Losing air should not overfill the tank</td></tr><tr><td>Cooling water to condenser</td><td>Fail open</td><td>Losing air should keep cooling on</td></tr><tr><td>Special service valve</td><td>Fail last</td><td>Holding position is safest for that process</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `Instrument air dropped at 0400. Operators expected the cooling water valve to shut and spent time trying to "open it back up," but it had failed open like it was designed to. The real problem was the feed valves slamming shut. Know your fail positions before the air goes.`,
    head: { q: 'Instrument air is lost. A fail-close valve does what?', a: 'Goes fully closed.' } },
  { title: 'Gate valves are not control valves',
    floor: `Try to set a trickle with a faucet that only has an on/off lever. It spits and chatters. A gate valve used half-open is like that, except the pipe is full of fast, dirty, maybe hot liquid.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="gate-valve">gate valve</span> is a flat gate that slides across the pipe. Fully open it has almost no restriction. Fully closed it seals. Part-open, most of the flow change happens in the last few turns, the gate vibrates, and the seats get cut by the fast flow. Once the seats are cut, it will not isolate when you really need it. For throttling, use a <span class="term" data-term="globe-valve">globe valve</span> or the <span class="term" data-term="control-valve">control valve</span>.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 170" role="img" aria-label="Gate valve partly open with fast flow cutting the seats"><path class="ln" d="M20 100 H500"/><rect class="eq" x="230" y="40" width="60" height="100"/><rect class="fillam" x="252" y="40" width="16" height="52"/><path class="red" d="M150 115 Q250 120 350 115"/><text x="260" y="160" text-anchor="middle" class="rt sm">fast flow under the gate wears the seat, then it will not seal</text><text x="260" y="30" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">gate half-open</text></svg>`,
    bite: `A plant ran a pump discharge gate valve "one turn open" for months to hold rate. When the pump needed a seal change, that gate would not seal. The job had to wait for a bigger shutdown. The temporary throttle cost a day of production later.`,
    head: { q: 'Why not throttle with a gate valve?', a: 'It controls poorly and fast flow cuts the seats, so later it will not isolate.' } },
  { title: 'Verifying valve position',
    floor: `You say you locked your car, but you still pull the handle to check. A valve's handle or indicator can say closed when it is not. Checking more than one way is normal, not paranoid.`,
    words: `Ways to verify: <strong>rising stem</strong> on many gate and globe valves, where the stem sticks out further when open. <strong>Position indicator</strong> on a ball, butterfly, or control valve (the handle across the pipe usually means closed). <strong>Count turns</strong> from full closed to full open and compare with what is normal for that valve. <strong>Process proof</strong>: pressure behind it, flow stops, a bleed shows no flow. For a <span class="term" data-term="control-valve">control valve</span>, compare the board output with the local stem travel indicator. For <span class="term" data-term="loto">LOTO</span>, follow the site verify steps.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Valve type</th><th>How to check</th><th>Watch out</th></tr><tr><td>Rising-stem gate / globe</td><td>Stem height, turns</td><td>Non-rising stem types show nothing outside</td></tr><tr><td>Ball / plug</td><td>Handle direction or indicator</td><td>Handle can be put on wrong or stem sheared</td></tr><tr><td>Butterfly</td><td>Indicator, gear box pointer</td><td>Pointer can slip on the shaft</td></tr><tr><td>Control valve</td><td>Local travel pointer vs board OP</td><td>Board OP is what was asked for, not proof it moved</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A ball valve handle was across the pipe, so it looked closed. Someone had reinstalled the handle 90° off after a repair. It was wide open. Pressure behind the flange proved it. That is why verify means more than one kind of evidence.`,
    head: { q: 'The board says FV-101 output is 0%. Does that prove the valve is closed?', a: 'No. OP is what was asked for. Check the local stem indicator and the process.' } }
];

STEPS.vacuum = [
  { title: 'Why plants pull vacuum',
    floor: `Water boils at a lower temperature on a mountain because there is less air pushing down on it. Campers notice their pasta takes longer. Plants use that same trick on purpose.`,
    words: `Pulling <span class="term" data-term="vacuum">vacuum</span> lowers the pressure, so liquids boil at a lower temperature. That lets a plant evaporate, distill, or dry product without cooking it. Other reasons: drying solids or equipment, pulling liquid from one vessel to another (vacuum transfer), de-gassing, and leak testing, where you pull vacuum and watch whether it holds. Every liquid has a <span class="term" data-term="vapor-pressure">vapor pressure</span>. Drop the pressure below it and the liquid boils.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Why pull vacuum</th><th>Plain version</th></tr><tr><td>Boil at lower temperature</td><td>Evaporate or separate without scorching the product</td></tr><tr><td>Dry</td><td>Pull water or solvent out of solids or equipment</td></tr><tr><td>Transfer</td><td>Let the outside air push liquid into the vacuum vessel</td></tr><tr><td>Leak test</td><td>Pull down, block in, and watch whether vacuum holds</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A dryer ran fine on days. At night the vacuum was a few inches weaker, so the solvent boiled at a higher temperature, and the operator raised the heat to "make it dry." The product discolored. Poor vacuum pushed the operator to change a second thing. Fix the vacuum, not the heat.`,
    head: { q: 'Why does pulling vacuum let a liquid boil at a lower temperature?', a: 'Less pressure on the liquid means it does not need as much heat to turn to vapor.' } },
  { title: 'Steam ejector vs liquid-ring or dry pump',
    floor: `Blow hard across the top of a straw standing in a glass of water and the water climbs the straw. Fast-moving steam does the same thing to gas in a pipe. That is a steam ejector. A vacuum pump is the other way to do it: a machine that scoops gas out.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="ejector">steam ejector</span> has no moving parts. High-pressure motive steam shoots through a nozzle, drags gas along with it, and discharges into a condenser or the atmosphere. It needs the right steam pressure and dry steam, and it hates high back pressure. A <span class="term" data-term="liquid-ring">liquid-ring pump</span> spins an off-center impeller in a ring of <span class="term" data-term="seal-water">seal water</span>. The water ring makes pockets that pull and squeeze gas. It needs cool seal water at the right flow. A <span class="term" data-term="dry-vacuum-pump">dry vacuum pump</span> uses close-fitting screws or lobes with no seal liquid. It hates liquid slugs and dirt.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th></th><th>Steam ejector</th><th>Liquid-ring pump</th><th>Dry pump</th></tr><tr><td>Moving parts</td><td>None</td><td>Impeller, motor</td><td>Screws or lobes, motor</td></tr><tr><td>Needs</td><td>Dry motive steam at design pressure, cooling water to the condenser</td><td>Cool seal water, steady flow</td><td>Clean dry gas, cooling, purge</td></tr><tr><td>Hurt by</td><td>Wet or low-pressure steam, high back pressure</td><td>Hot seal water, low seal flow</td><td>Liquid slugs, solids</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `On a winter night, vacuum drifted down slowly. Steam pressure to the ejector looked normal, but a steam trap upstream had failed and wet steam was hitting the nozzle. Ejectors hate wet steam. Draining the steam line brought vacuum back.`,
    head: { q: 'A liquid-ring pump\'s seal water is running hot. What happens to vacuum?', a: 'It gets worse. Hot seal water flashes and the pump cannot pull as deep.' } },
  { title: 'Suction side and discharge side',
    floor: `A shop vacuum has a hose end (suction) and an exhaust port (discharge). Plug the exhaust and it stops pulling. Plant vacuum systems care about both ends too.`,
    words: `The suction side is connected to the process vessel you want at vacuum, often through a <span class="term" data-term="condenser">condenser</span> and a <span class="term" data-term="knockout-pot">knockout pot</span>. The board usually shows suction vacuum on a PI or <span class="term" data-term="pic">PIC</span>. The discharge side goes to the atmosphere through a vent, silencer, or scrubber, or into another condenser or separator. If the discharge gets restricted (a plugged vent, a flooded separator, a closed valve), the vacuum device cannot push gas out, and suction vacuum falls.`,
    pic: SYSTEM_SVG + `<p class="fig-note">VE-101 receiver → condenser E-101 → knockout pot KO-101 → vacuum pump VP-101 → vent/separator. Tap a symbol for Plant Mentor.</p>`,
    bite: `Vacuum fell every time the scrubber downstream was backwashed. The pump discharge went to the scrubber, and backwash raised the back pressure. Nobody connected the two until someone overlaid the trends. Look downstream, not just upstream.`,
    head: { q: 'The vent on the vacuum pump discharge gets plugged. Does suction vacuum get better or worse?', a: 'Worse. The pump cannot push the gas out.' } },
  { title: 'Air leaks: how the board lies',
    floor: `Suck on a straw with a pin hole in the side. You pull harder and harder but get less. Your cheeks tell you you are working, but the drink does not come. A vacuum system with an <span class="term" data-term="air-leak">air leak</span> feels the same.`,
    words: `Under vacuum, leaks go <strong>inward</strong>. You will not see a drip. You may hear a hiss at a gasket, valve packing, sight glass, or flange. The air load fills the vacuum device, so suction vacuum falls. A <span class="term" data-term="pic">PIC</span> that bleeds air or gas in to hold vacuum may close its bleed all the way and still not reach setpoint. Condenser outlet temperatures may look fine because the air does not condense. Air also blankets condenser tubes, making cooling look poor. A leak test (block in and watch vacuum rise) shows the leak rate.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Board shows</th><th>Could be an air leak if…</th></tr><tr><td>Vacuum slowly falling</td><td>Motive steam, seal water, and cooling look normal</td></tr><tr><td>PIC bleed valve at 0% and still not at setpoint</td><td>Something else is letting gas in</td></tr><tr><td>Condenser seems weak</td><td>Air is blanketing the tubes</td></tr><tr><td>Block-in test: vacuum rises fast</td><td>Leak rate is high</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `After a maintenance job, vacuum would not come down to normal. Everyone chased the ejector steam for two hours. An operator walking the unit with a flashlight heard a hiss: the new sight glass gasket was pinched. The board looked like an ejector problem. The truth was a $5 gasket.`,
    head: { q: 'Under vacuum, which way does a leak go?', a: 'Inward. Air gets sucked in, so you may hear a hiss instead of seeing a drip.' } },
  { title: 'Seal water, knockout pot, condenser: what saves you',
    floor: `Think of a kitchen range hood with a grease trap. If the trap fills up, grease goes where it should not. The small parts around a vacuum system decide whether the big machine works.`,
    words: `The <span class="term" data-term="condenser">condenser</span> turns vapor back into liquid with cooling water. That makes much less gas for the vacuum device to handle, so the cooling water flow and temperature matter a lot. The <span class="term" data-term="knockout-pot">knockout pot</span> catches liquid drops before they reach the vacuum pump. If its level gets high or its drain plugs, liquid gets carried over into the pump. <span class="term" data-term="seal-water">Seal water</span> on a liquid-ring pump must be cool and at the right flow. Most "vacuum problems" are one of these three.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Saver</th><th>Healthy</th><th>When it fails</th></tr><tr><td>Condenser</td><td>Cooling water flowing, normal temperatures</td><td>More vapor to the pump, vacuum falls</td></tr><tr><td>Knockout pot</td><td>Low level, drain working</td><td>Liquid carryover floods the pump, vacuum falls</td></tr><tr><td>Seal water</td><td>Cool, steady flow</td><td>Water flashes in the ring, vacuum falls</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `At 0100 vacuum fell and the knockout pot level alarm came in. The pot's drain valve to the seal pot had been closed for a filter change on the day shift and never reopened. Liquid carried over into the liquid-ring pump. Opening the drain fixed it. Check the small valves after any job.`,
    head: { q: 'Knockout pot level is high and vacuum is falling. What is likely happening?', a: 'Liquid is carrying over into the vacuum pump. Check the pot drain.' } },
  { title: '"We lost vacuum": first five checks',
    floor: `When your car will not start, you check the simple things first: fuel, battery, lights. You do not pull the engine. Losing vacuum works the same way. Check the supplies before you blame the machine.`,
    words: `Use a steady order so you do not change three things at once. Your site procedure comes first. This is a thinking order for training. <strong>1. Motive:</strong> steam pressure to the <span class="term" data-term="ejector">ejector</span>, or seal water flow and temperature to the <span class="term" data-term="liquid-ring">liquid-ring pump</span>. Is the pump running? <strong>2. Cooling:</strong> condenser cooling water flow and temperature. <strong>3. Knockout pot:</strong> level and drain. <strong>4. Discharge path:</strong> vent, separator, or back pressure. <strong>5. Leaks and load:</strong> recent work, open valves, gaskets, hissing, or a big change in process vapor. See the <span class="term" data-term="lost-vacuum">lost-vacuum checklist</span> in Plant Mentor.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Check</th><th>Board clue</th><th>Field clue</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Motive steam / seal water / pump running</td><td>Steam PI, seal water FI, amps</td><td>Gauge at the ejector, seal water flow, pump hum</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Condenser cooling</td><td>CW temperatures, CW flow</td><td>Warm return line, CW valve position</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Knockout pot</td><td>Level alarm</td><td>Sight glass, drain line warm or cold</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Discharge path</td><td>Back pressure</td><td>Vent flow, separator level</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Leaks / load</td><td>PIC bleed at 0%</td><td>Hiss, recent work, open drains</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `Vacuum dropped fast at shift change. One person raised steam, another opened the PIC bleed, a third cut feed, all within five minutes. Nobody could tell what fixed it or what made it worse. The real cause was cooling water: a CW pump had tripped. Go in order, one change at a time, and say what you are doing on the radio.`,
    head: { q: 'Name the first thing on the "lost vacuum" check list.', a: 'The motive: steam to the ejector or seal water to the liquid-ring pump (and is the pump running).' } }
];

STEPS.filtration = [
  { title: 'What a filter is really doing',
    floor: `A coffee filter holds back the grounds and lets the coffee through. A window screen stops bugs but not air. Plant filters do those same jobs, just with pressure behind them.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="filter">filter</span> does one or more of three jobs: <strong>hold dirt</strong> (rust, scale, rags, weld slag), <strong>protect</strong> what is downstream (pump seals, spray nozzles, heat exchanger tubes, the product), and <strong>recover solids</strong> when the solids are the product. Knowing which job your filter does tells you how much a bypass or a torn element will hurt.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Job</th><th>Example</th><th>What a bypass costs</th></tr><tr><td>Hold dirt</td><td>Suction strainer ahead of P-101</td><td>Rags into the pump impeller</td></tr><tr><td>Protect</td><td>Cartridge filter ahead of spray nozzles</td><td>Plugged nozzles, off-spec product</td></tr><tr><td>Recover solids</td><td>Leaf or rotary filter catching crystals</td><td>Product lost down the drain</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A filter on the feed to an exchanger was bypassed "for one shift" when DP got high. The exchanger tubes plugged over the next week, and the unit had to come down for cleaning. The filter was cheap. The exchanger downtime was not.`,
    head: { q: 'Name the three jobs a filter can do.', a: 'Hold dirt, protect equipment or product downstream, and recover solids.' } },
  { title: 'Strainer, cartridge, bag, and bigger process filters',
    floor: `A kitchen sink has a basket to catch big food chunks. A water pitcher has a fine cartridge. A vacuum cleaner has a bag. Each catches a different size of dirt, and plants use all three ideas.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="strainer">strainer</span> is a coarse screen (Y-type or basket) that catches big stuff to protect pumps and valves. A <span class="term" data-term="cartridge-filter">cartridge filter</span> uses replaceable tubes for finer particles. A <span class="term" data-term="bag-filter">bag filter</span> uses a fabric bag in a basket, good for moderate dirt loads. Larger process filters include leaf or plate filters (screens or cloths that build up a cake of solids), rotary drum filters (a turning drum under vacuum that picks up and scrapes off a cake), and others. Duplex (twin) housings let you switch to a clean side without stopping flow.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Catches</th><th>Operator job</th></tr><tr><td>Y or basket strainer</td><td>Big debris</td><td>Watch DP, clean basket per procedure</td></tr><tr><td>Cartridge</td><td>Fine particles</td><td>Change elements at DP limit</td></tr><tr><td>Bag</td><td>Moderate load</td><td>Change bags, check seal ring seated</td></tr><tr><td>Leaf / plate</td><td>Solids cake, often with filter aid</td><td>Precoat, run, blow down, clean</td></tr><tr><td>Rotary drum (vacuum)</td><td>Continuous cake</td><td>Watch vacuum, cake thickness, wash</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A bag was changed and the new bag's ring was not seated in the basket. DP looked great and very low all night. Unfiltered liquid was going around the bag. A "too good" DP right after a change means go check the seal.`,
    head: { q: 'After a bag change, DP is much lower than any clean bag you have seen. What do you suspect?', a: 'The bag is not seated or is torn, so flow is going around it.' } },
  { title: 'DP is the operator\'s truth',
    floor: `You can tell your furnace filter is dirty without opening it: the house airflow gets weak. Filter DP is the plant's way of telling you the same thing without opening anything.`,
    words: `Write down the <strong>clean DP</strong> right after a change at normal flow. That is your baseline. As dirt builds, <span class="term" data-term="filter-dp">filter DP</span> climbs. Your site will have a change-out or clean limit. Remember that DP also rises with more flow, so compare at similar rates. The <span class="term" data-term="bypass">bypass</span> temptation: when DP is high and rate is suffering, opening the bypass valve makes the numbers look better immediately. It also sends unfiltered liquid downstream. Only bypass if the procedure allows it and the right person agrees.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 200" role="img" aria-label="Filter DP trend rising over time, then dropping after a change"><path class="th" d="M50 170 H490 M50 170 V20"/><text x="45" y="30" text-anchor="end" class="sm">DP</text><text x="480" y="190" class="sm" text-anchor="end">time →</text><path class="amb" d="M50 150 C150 148 220 140 280 110 S360 45 380 40 L385 150 L490 148"/><path class="red" d="M50 60 H490" stroke-dasharray="6 5"/><text x="60" y="55" class="rt sm">example change-out limit</text><text x="60" y="140" class="gt sm">clean baseline</text><text x="395" y="140" class="gt sm">after change</text></svg>`,
    bite: `A night crew opened the filter bypass "just to get through the shift." The next crew did not know, and the bypass stayed open four days. Downstream spray nozzles plugged and off-spec product went to storage. If anything is bypassed, it goes on the turnover log and gets a tag.`,
    head: { q: 'Filter DP is up but flow was also raised 30%. Is the filter necessarily dirtier?', a: 'Not necessarily. More flow gives more DP too. Compare at similar flow.' } },
  { title: 'Precoat and body feed (filter aid)',
    floor: `Think of making a sand bed at the bottom of a fish tank filter. The sand does the fine filtering and the screen just holds the sand. Some plant filters work that way.`,
    words: `Some chemical plants use a filter aid, a fine powder like diatomaceous earth or cellulose. <span class="term" data-term="precoat">Precoat</span> means building a thin layer of filter aid on the screens or cloths before the real product goes through. The layer does the fine filtering. <span class="term" data-term="body-feed">Body feed</span> means adding a little filter aid into the dirty liquid while running, so the cake stays open and does not blind (seal off) too fast. If precoat is patchy, dirty liquid gets through. If body feed stops, DP climbs fast.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 190" role="img" aria-label="Screen with precoat layer and cake building on top"><rect class="eq" x="60" y="130" width="400" height="12"/><text x="470" y="140" class="sm">screen</text><rect x="60" y="112" width="400" height="18" fill="#c9b88a"/><text x="470" y="125" class="sm">precoat</text><rect x="60" y="80" width="400" height="32" fill="#6b5a3a"/><text x="470" y="100" class="sm">cake (solids + body feed)</text><path class="ln" d="M260 20 V70"/><path d="M252 60 L260 76 L268 60" fill="#d6dde4"/><text x="275" y="40" class="sm">dirty liquid in</text><path class="ln" d="M260 145 V180"/><text x="275" y="178" class="sm">clean filtrate out</text></svg>`,
    bite: `The body feed pump lost prime at 0300 and nobody noticed. Filter DP on the leaf filter went up three times faster than normal. The crew thought they got a bad batch of feed. Check the body feed rate when DP climbs fast.`,
    head: { q: 'What is precoat?', a: 'A thin layer of filter aid put on the screen or cloth before filtering, so the layer does the fine filtering.' } },
  { title: 'Swap/clean, or is DP telling you something else?',
    floor: `If your hose flow drops, it might be a kink, a closed spigot, or a clog in the nozzle. You do not buy a new nozzle until you check the kink. High filter DP has lookalikes too.`,
    words: `High DP with low flow usually means a loaded filter. Before you swap, check: <strong>is flow higher than normal?</strong> (DP rises with flow). <strong>Is a valve pinched</strong> on the filter inlet or outlet, or is the wrong valve lined up on a duplex? <strong>Is the PDT reading true</strong>, or is an impulse line plugged? Low DP with low flow points somewhere else: a dying pump, a closed valve upstream, or a plugged suction <span class="term" data-term="strainer">strainer</span>. A sudden drop in DP can mean a torn element or an open bypass.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>DP</th><th>Flow</th><th>Pump discharge</th><th>Likely</th></tr><tr><td>High</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td><td>Filter loaded. Plan swap/clean.</td></tr><tr><td>High</td><td>High</td><td>Normal</td><td>More flow than usual. Filter may be fine.</td></tr><tr><td>Low</td><td>Low</td><td>Low</td><td>Pump or suction problem, not the filter</td></tr><tr><td>Sudden low</td><td>Back up</td><td>Normal</td><td>Torn element or bypass open</td></tr><tr><td>Flat, not moving at all</td><td>Changing</td><td>Changing</td><td>Suspect the PDT or its lines</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A crew swapped a cartridge filter three times in one night because flow kept dropping. DP was actually <em>low</em>. The pump's suction strainer was plugged. Three good cartridges went in the trash. Look at DP and pump pressures together before you swap.`,
    head: { q: 'Flow is low and filter DP is low too. Is the filter the problem?', a: 'Probably not. Look upstream: pump, suction strainer, valves.' } },
  { title: 'Never open a filter under pressure',
    floor: `Opening a shaken soda bottle fast sprays everywhere. A filter housing is a big soda bottle full of process liquid, maybe hot, maybe hazardous. The lid bolts are holding all of that back.`,
    words: `Before opening any filter housing or strainer, follow your procedure. In general: <span class="term" data-term="isolation">isolate</span> inlet and outlet, drain, vent, and verify <span class="term" data-term="zero-energy">zero pressure</span> with more than one indication: the gauge reads zero, the vent is open and quiet, and the drain is not flowing. A plugged vent or a clogged gauge can hide pressure. Loosen bolts in a way that lets the lid crack safely first, never all the way off at once. Wear the PPE your site requires. On a duplex filter, confirm the in-service side before touching the other side.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Proof</th></tr><tr><td>Isolate inlet and outlet</td><td>Valves verified closed, locked if the procedure says so</td></tr><tr><td>Drain</td><td>Drain flow stops</td></tr><tr><td>Vent</td><td>Vent open, no hiss, no flow</td></tr><tr><td>Verify zero</td><td>Gauge at zero AND vent open and quiet. Do not trust one gauge.</td></tr><tr><td>Open carefully</td><td>Lid cracked with bolts still engaged, then fully open</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `An operator opened a strainer lid after the gauge read zero. The gauge tap was plugged with solids. The housing still held pressure, and hot liquid sprayed out when the lid lifted. Verify zero more than one way, every time.`,
    head: { q: 'The gauge reads zero on a strainer. Is that enough to open it?', a: 'No. Also confirm the vent is open and quiet and the drain has stopped. Gauges plug.' } }
];

STEPS.together = [
  { title: 'One system story',
    floor: `Think of a garden hose connected to a spigot, running through a sprayer, into a bucket. If the sprayer clogs, you notice less water in the bucket, the hose gets stiff, and the spigot sounds different. One problem shows up in several places.`,
    words: `Our system: liquid leaves feed tank T-101, pump P-101 pushes it through filter F-101, the control valve FV-101 (run by <span class="term" data-term="fic">FIC-101</span>) sets flow, and it lands in vacuum receiver VE-101. The receiver's vapor goes through condenser E-101 and <span class="term" data-term="knockout-pot">knockout pot</span> KO-101 to the <span class="term" data-term="liquid-ring">liquid-ring pump</span> VP-101. <span class="term" data-term="pic">PIC-101</span> holds receiver vacuum. Every piece talks to its neighbors.`,
    pic: SYSTEM_SVG + `<p class="fig-note">Tap any symbol for Plant Mentor.</p>`,
    bite: `A night operator only watched the vacuum screen. Feed flow had been drifting low for two hours because of a filter, so less liquid flashed in the receiver. Vacuum looked "better than ever." It was a clue, not a win.`,
    head: { q: 'Where does the liquid go after FV-101 in this story?', a: 'Into vacuum receiver VE-101.' } },
  { title: 'A dirty filter changes everything downstream',
    floor: `When the sprayer on your hose clogs, the hose stiffens (more pressure behind), less water comes out, and the bucket fills slower. One clog, three symptoms.`,
    words: `As F-101 loads, <span class="term" data-term="pdt">PDT-101</span> rises. Pump discharge pressure PIT-102 creeps up because the pump is pushing against more restriction. On a centrifugal pump, less flow usually means lower <span class="term" data-term="amps">amps</span>. <span class="term" data-term="fic">FIC-101</span> sees flow dropping and opens FV-101 further to compensate, until FV-101 reaches 100% and cannot give more. Then flow falls below setpoint. Less liquid reaches VE-101, so its level drops and there is less vapor, which can change the vacuum.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Reading</th><th>What it does as F-101 plugs</th></tr><tr><td>PDT-101 (filter DP)</td><td>Rises</td></tr><tr><td>PIT-102 (pump discharge)</td><td>Rises a bit</td></tr><tr><td>P-101 amps (centrifugal)</td><td>Falls a bit (less flow)</td></tr><tr><td>FIC-101 OP to FV-101</td><td>Opens more, then pins at 100%</td></tr><tr><td>Flow</td><td>Held at setpoint, then falls</td></tr><tr><td>VE-101 level / vacuum</td><td>Level falls, vacuum can shift because of less vapor load</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `The board alarm said "FV-101 at 100% output." The night crew called instruments to "fix the stuck valve." The valve was fine. It was wide open because the filter was plugged. A controller output pinned at 100% is a clue to look upstream.`,
    head: { q: 'FV-101 output creeps up to 100% while PDT-101 climbs. What is the root cause?', a: 'The filter F-101 is loading up. The valve is opening to make up for it.' } },
  { title: 'Downstream: vacuum and level answer back',
    floor: `If you pour water into a pan on a hot stove, the steam cloud depends on how much water you pour. Pour less, and there is less steam. The vacuum side of a plant feels what the liquid side sends it.`,
    words: `In VE-101, part of the incoming liquid flashes into vapor because the pressure is low. The condenser turns that vapor back into liquid, and VP-101 pulls away whatever does not condense, including air from leaks. Less feed means less vapor, so vacuum may look deeper. More feed, or hotter feed, means more vapor, so vacuum may fall. A <span class="term" data-term="lic">level controller</span> on the receiver will adjust its outlet to hold level. Knowing this, a vacuum change can be a <em>result</em> of a liquid-side change, not a vacuum-system problem.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Liquid side change</th><th>Vacuum side answer</th></tr><tr><td>Feed flow drops (filter plugging)</td><td>Less vapor → vacuum may deepen. Receiver level falls.</td></tr><tr><td>Feed gets hotter</td><td>More flash vapor → vacuum may fall</td></tr><tr><td>Cooling water to E-101 drops</td><td>Vapor not condensed → vacuum falls</td></tr><tr><td>KO-101 level high</td><td>Liquid carryover → VP-101 struggles → vacuum falls</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `Vacuum on VE-101 dropped every afternoon when the feed tank got warmer in the sun. Night shift thought the vacuum pump was wearing out because the problem showed up by the time they came in. The fix was upstream cooling. The vacuum pump was fine.`,
    head: { q: 'Feed flow into the receiver dropped. What might vacuum do?', a: 'It may get deeper because there is less vapor to handle.' } },
  { title: 'What you look at first',
    floor: `If three lights come on in your car at once, you do not start fixing all three. You look for the one that caused the others, often the battery or the alternator. Plants reward the same patience.`,
    words: `When several numbers move: <strong>1.</strong> Make it safe. Follow alarms and the upset procedure. <strong>2.</strong> Look at trends and find <strong>what moved first</strong>. <strong>3.</strong> Pair readings to confirm it is real (DP with flow, amps with pressure, level with flow). <strong>4.</strong> Make <strong>one</strong> change, say it on the radio, and wait long enough to see the answer. <strong>5.</strong> Log what you did and hand it off. Do not flip a controller to <span class="term" data-term="manual-mode">manual</span> and walk away, and never defeat an <span class="term" data-term="interlock">interlock</span> to make rate.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 210" role="img" aria-label="Trend with filter DP rising first, then flow falling, then vacuum changing"><path class="th" d="M40 190 H500 M40 190 V20"/><path class="amb" d="M40 150 H150 C200 150 230 80 300 70 H500"/><text x="505" y="70" class="am sm" text-anchor="end">PDT-101</text><path class="grn" d="M40 60 H250 C290 60 320 120 360 125 H500"/><text x="505" y="118" class="gt sm" text-anchor="end">flow</text><path class="th" d="M40 170 H330 C360 170 390 150 420 150 H500" stroke="#7cc4ff"/><text x="505" y="145" class="bt sm" text-anchor="end">vacuum</text><path class="red" d="M150 30 V190" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="155" y="40" class="rt sm">moved first</text></svg>`,
    bite: `During an upset, an operator changed FIC-101 setpoint, PIC-101 setpoint, and the cooling water valve inside ten minutes. The unit settled, but nobody learned why it upset, and it happened again the next night. One change at a time lets you find the real cause.`,
    head: { q: 'Filter DP rose first, then flow fell, then vacuum changed. What do you address first?', a: 'The filter: the first thing that moved.' } }
];

STEPS.loto = [
  { title: 'Why energy has to be controlled',
    floor: `Before you change a light fixture at home, you flip the breaker, and then you check with a tester that the wire is dead. You do not trust the switch on the wall. Plant equipment has many more kinds of energy than one wire.`,
    words: `<span class="term" data-term="loto">LOTO</span> (lockout/tagout) is how a site controls hazardous energy so equipment cannot start, move, or release something while people work on it. Energy is more than electricity: <strong>pressure</strong> (liquid, gas, steam), <strong>heat</strong>, <strong>chemical</strong>, <strong>stored mechanical</strong> (springs, a raised weight, a flywheel still spinning), and <strong>gravity</strong> (liquid in a line above you). <em>This lesson is support training. Your site LOTO procedure and permits always come first.</em>`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Energy</th><th>Pump P-101 example</th></tr><tr><td>Electrical</td><td>Motor breaker</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure</td><td>Discharge line still pressurized from the header</td></tr><tr><td>Liquid / gravity</td><td>Tank T-101 above the suction</td></tr><tr><td>Thermal</td><td>Hot product in the casing</td></tr><tr><td>Chemical</td><td>Hazardous liquid in the casing and seal area</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanical</td><td>Shaft can spin backward if flow comes back through</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A motor was locked out, and the mechanic started unbolting the pump casing. Nobody had closed the discharge block valve. Header pressure pushed liquid backward through the pump. Electrical isolation alone is not isolation.`,
    head: { q: 'Name three kinds of energy besides electrical that could be in a pump.', a: 'Pressure, heat, chemical, stored mechanical, gravity (any three).' } },
  { title: 'Isolation types',
    floor: `To stop water to one sink, you can close the valve under it. To be really sure for a long job, a plumber may cap the pipe. The more serious the job, the stronger the isolation.`,
    words: `<span class="term" data-term="isolation">Isolation</span> separates the equipment from every energy source. Common types, weakest to strongest: a single block valve; <span class="term" data-term="bleed">double block and bleed</span> (two closed valves with an open bleed between them, so any leak-by drains out instead of reaching you); and positive isolation with a <span class="term" data-term="blind">blind</span> (a solid plate) or a removed spool with caps. Your site procedure says which one each job needs. Control valves and check valves are not isolation.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 560 170" role="img" aria-label="Double block and bleed arrangement"><path class="ln" d="M20 90 H540"/><path class="eq" d="M150 75 L180 105 L180 75 L150 105 z"/><path class="eq" d="M330 75 L360 105 L360 75 L330 105 z"/><path class="th" d="M255 90 V135"/><path class="eq" d="M245 135 L265 155 L265 135 L245 155 z" transform="rotate(90 255 145)"/><text x="165" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">block 1 (closed)</text><text x="345" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">block 2 (closed)</text><text x="275" y="150" class="gt sm">bleed open: proves no leak-by</text><text x="40" y="80" class="sm">energy side</text><text x="450" y="80" class="sm">work side</text></svg>`,
    bite: `A single gate valve was used to isolate a steam line for a gasket change. The gate had cut seats from being throttled for months. Steam leaked through and burned a worker's hand. The procedure required double block and bleed on that service for exactly this reason.`,
    head: { q: 'In double block and bleed, why is the bleed open?', a: 'So anything leaking past the first block drains out and shows itself, instead of building pressure on the second valve.' } },
  { title: 'Lock, tag, and control the keys',
    floor: `If you lock your bike, only your key opens it. That is the whole idea of a personal lock. Nobody else can undo your safety.`,
    words: `After isolation points are set, each person working applies their own lock (often through a group lockbox) and a tag with their name. The lock stops the device from being operated. The tag tells people why and who. Rules that nearly every site shares: only the person who put a lock on removes it (except for a formal, approved removal process); never work under someone else's lock; and shift changes need a clear handoff. Your site's permit and LOTO procedure give the exact steps.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Do</th><th>Don't</th></tr><tr><td>Put your own lock on (or on the lockbox)</td><td>Work under someone else's lock</td></tr><tr><td>Check every isolation point on the list</td><td>Assume the last shift did it</td></tr><tr><td>Hand off at shift change per procedure</td><td>Remove a lock that is not yours</td></tr><tr><td>Stop and ask if anything is different</td><td>"Make it fit" when the list does not match the field</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A night crew needed a pump back fast. A lock was still on it from day shift, and the owner had gone home. Someone suggested cutting it. The supervisor stopped them and followed the site's formal lock-removal process. It took an hour. That hour is cheaper than a funeral.`,
    head: { q: 'Can you remove another person\'s lock because they went home?', a: 'No. Only through your site\'s formal, approved removal process.' } },
  { title: 'Verify zero energy',
    floor: `After you flip the breaker, you use a tester on the wire. After you lock out a pump, you still try to start it and check the pressure is gone. That check is the step people skip, and it is the one that saves lives.`,
    words: `Verify <span class="term" data-term="zero-energy">zero energy</span> means proving every source is actually off, not just switched off. Examples: try the start button (the try-step) and confirm nothing moves, then return it to off; open vents and drains and confirm they are quiet and not flowing; read gauges, and do not trust one gauge; confirm temperature is safe; confirm moving parts have stopped. If anything is not at zero, stop and find out why before work starts.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Source</th><th>How to verify (general)</th></tr><tr><td>Electrical</td><td>Try-start, local and remote. Qualified people test for voltage where required.</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure</td><td>Vent open and quiet, drain stopped, gauge zero</td></tr><tr><td>Heat</td><td>Temperature check, cool-down time</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanical</td><td>Shaft stopped, springs released, weights lowered or blocked</td></tr><tr><td>Chemical</td><td>Drained, flushed, purged per procedure</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A pump was locked out at the wrong breaker, one labeled for its twin. The try-step would have caught it. The crew skipped it because "we always get it right." The motor bumped when the other crew started what they thought was their pump. Always try-start.`,
    head: { q: 'Why do a try-start after locking out?', a: 'To prove the right energy source is really off. Labels and breakers can be wrong.' } },
  { title: 'Trapped energy and returning to service',
    floor: `You close both ends of a garden hose left in the sun. Later, you open one end and it sprays. The water inside heated up and built pressure. Blocked-in lines do that too.`,
    words: `Energy can hide between closed valves: liquid that heats up and expands, a pocket of gas, a line higher than you that drains when opened. Check valves can hold pressure on one side. Filters and strainers hold liquid and pressure. When the work is done, returning to service is also a controlled step: people clear, tools out, guards on, locks removed by their owners, lines filled and vented slowly, and leaks checked. Rushing the return has hurt as many people as rushing the start.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Hidden energy</th><th>Where it hides</th></tr><tr><td>Thermal expansion</td><td>Liquid blocked between two valves in the sun or near heat</td></tr><tr><td>Trapped pressure</td><td>Downstream of a check valve, inside filter housings</td></tr><tr><td>Gravity</td><td>Liquid in overhead lines</td></tr><tr><td>Stored mechanical</td><td>Springs in actuators, fail-open or fail-close valves</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A control valve actuator was unbolted with the valve isolated and the air supply locked off. The spring inside was still compressed. The top came off hard. Stored spring energy is energy. Know which parts hold it.`,
    head: { q: 'Name one place energy can be trapped after isolation.', a: 'Between two closed valves, behind a check valve, in a filter housing, in overhead lines, or in an actuator spring.' } }
];

STEPS.failures = [
  { title: 'Plugged strainer',
    floor: `A sink drain slows down as hair and gunk build up. It does not fail all at once. It gets a little slower every day until it stops.`,
    words: `A plugged suction <span class="term" data-term="strainer">strainer</span> starves the pump. Signs: suction pressure falls (it may go to vacuum), strainer DP rises if there is a gauge, discharge pressure and flow drop, amps drop, and the pump may start to <span class="term" data-term="cavitation">cavitate</span>. Plugging often gets worse after a tank cleaning, a new line startup, or a change in feed.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Reading</th><th>Plugged suction strainer</th></tr><tr><td>Suction pressure</td><td>Down, maybe vacuum</td></tr><tr><td>Strainer DP</td><td>Up</td></tr><tr><td>Discharge pressure / flow</td><td>Down, maybe jumpy</td></tr><tr><td>Amps</td><td>Down</td></tr><tr><td>Sound</td><td>Gravel if cavitating</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A new line was started after construction. The pump strainer caught welding rods, gloves, and tape within the first hour. The pump cavitated until someone checked the strainer. After new work, expect strainers to plug fast.`,
    head: { q: 'Suction pressure is falling and amps are dropping. First suspect?', a: 'A plugged suction strainer (or low level / pinched suction valve).' } },
  { title: 'Passing isolation valve',
    floor: `An old faucet that still drips when turned off hard is "passing." It is closed, but it does not seal.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="passing-valve">passing valve</span> leaks through while closed. Causes: cut seats from throttling (especially <span class="term" data-term="gate-valve">gates</span>), debris in the seat, a warped disk, or not fully closed. Signs: a level that creeps in a tank that should be blocked in, a warm line that should be cold, pressure building between two closed valves, or flow at a bleed. This is why <span class="term" data-term="bleed">double block and bleed</span> and verify steps exist.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Clue</th><th>Could mean</th></tr><tr><td>Blocked-in tank level slowly rising</td><td>Inlet valve passing</td></tr><tr><td>Bleed between blocks keeps flowing</td><td>First block passing</td></tr><tr><td>"Isolated" line warm to the hand held near it</td><td>Hot product leaking through</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure returns after venting</td><td>Something upstream is passing</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A tank was blocked in for inspection. Overnight the level rose 6 inches. The operator logged "level transmitter drift." It was a passing inlet valve. Verifying with a second level indication would have shown it was real.`,
    head: { q: 'A blocked-in tank level keeps creeping up. What do you check?', a: 'Whether an inlet valve is passing, and confirm the level is real with a second indication.' } },
  { title: 'Failed-open control valve and stuck check valve',
    floor: `A car's gas pedal stuck to the floor is scary because the car keeps going no matter what you want. A control valve stuck open is the same kind of problem. A door that will not swing shut is the check-valve version.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="control-valve">control valve</span> can stick open from a broken stem, a failed positioner, or loss of signal on a <span class="term" data-term="fail-position">fail-open</span> valve. Signs: the controller output goes to 0% but flow stays high; the local stem pointer does not match the board. Use the upstream block valve or procedure to take control. A <span class="term" data-term="check-valve">check valve</span> can stick open (backflow when the pump stops, pump spins backward) or stick closed (no flow even though the pump runs, discharge pressure high, like a <span class="term" data-term="dead-head">dead-head</span>).`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Failure</th><th>Board clue</th><th>Field clue</th></tr><tr><td>Control valve stuck open</td><td>OP at 0%, flow still high</td><td>Stem pointer does not move</td></tr><tr><td>Check stuck open</td><td>Flow reverses when pump stops</td><td>Pump shaft spins backward</td></tr><tr><td>Check stuck closed</td><td>Pump running, no flow, high discharge</td><td>Hot pump casing, no flow noise</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A spare pump was started and the running pump stopped. The stopped pump's check valve stuck open, and liquid from the new pump ran backward through it. Flow to the unit barely changed. The spare looked broken until someone saw the other shaft turning backward.`,
    head: { q: 'Controller output is 0% but flow is still high. What do you suspect?', a: 'The control valve is stuck or failed open (or there is an open bypass). Check the stem in the field.' } },
  { title: 'Air leak on vacuum',
    floor: `A juice box with a hole near the top: you squeeze and suck, but you pull air instead of juice. Vacuum systems fail quietly the same way.`,
    words: `An <span class="term" data-term="air-leak">air leak</span> on a vacuum system lets outside air in through gaskets, packing, sight glasses, instrument fittings, or open drains. Vacuum falls slowly, the vacuum pump works harder, and the condenser gets blanketed with air. You will not see a drip. Listen for hissing, and check recently opened spots first. A block-in leak test measures how fast vacuum rises.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Likely leak spot</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Anything touched on the last job</td><td>Gaskets pinched, bolts not torqued</td></tr><tr><td>Valve packing</td><td>Packing that seals pressure may leak under vacuum</td></tr><tr><td>Sight glasses, instrument fittings</td><td>Small, many, easy to miss</td></tr><tr><td>Open drain or sample valve</td><td>Left open after a sample</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `After a sample at 0200, the sample valve on the receiver was left cracked. Vacuum drifted down all night. Day shift found it in five minutes by checking "what did we touch last?" Start with the last thing opened.`,
    head: { q: 'After a job, vacuum will not come down to normal. Where do you look first?', a: 'At whatever was opened or touched during the job: gaskets, fittings, drains.' } },
  { title: 'Blown rupture disk vs lifting relief valve',
    floor: `A pressure cooker has a weighted valve that jiggles to let steam out, and then settles. It also has a backup plug that pops out for good if things get really bad. Plants use the same two ideas.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="relief-valve">relief valve</span> is spring-loaded. It opens (lifts) when pressure reaches its set point and should close again when pressure drops, though it may not seal perfectly afterward. A <span class="term" data-term="rupture-disk">rupture disk</span> is a thin metal disk that bursts at a set pressure and stays open. It must be replaced. Signs of a relief event: noise, flow to the flare or vent header, a burst-disk alarm or indicator, or pressure dropping suddenly. Either one means pressure got too high. Report it, get the area safe, and do not isolate or tamper with the device.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th></th><th>Relief valve</th><th>Rupture disk</th></tr><tr><td>Opens</td><td>At set pressure</td><td>Bursts at set pressure</td></tr><tr><td>Afterward</td><td>Recloses (may leak after)</td><td>Stays open until replaced</td></tr><tr><td>Operator move</td><td>Find why pressure rose. Report.</td><td>Unit may need to come down safely. Report.</td></tr><tr><td>Never</td><td colspan="2">Block in, gag, or adjust it to keep running</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A relief on a pump discharge lifted twice on nights. Someone suggested "closing the block valve under the relief so it stops dumping." That would have removed the only protection. The real problem was a closed downstream valve. Relief lifting is a symptom. Find the cause.`,
    head: { q: 'What is the main difference after a relief valve lifts vs a rupture disk bursts?', a: 'A relief valve can reclose. A rupture disk stays open until it is replaced.' } }
];

STEPS.instruments = [
  { title: 'The DP cell manifold, done wrong',
    floor: `Picture a see-saw with a kid on each end. If one kid jumps off suddenly, the other slams down. A DP cell has a high side and a low side, and opening or closing them in the wrong order slams it the same way.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="dp-cell">DP cell</span> usually has a 3-valve or 5-valve <span class="term" data-term="manifold">manifold</span>: a high-side block, a low-side block, and an equalizing valve between them (5-valve adds vents). The equalizer, when open, makes both sides see the same pressure. Doing it in the wrong order can put full line pressure on one side only, which can damage the cell or shift its calibration. It also swings the reading, which can move a controller or trip an <span class="term" data-term="interlock">interlock</span>. Manifold work is usually for instrument techs or trained operators with the controller in manual.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 200" role="img" aria-label="Three valve manifold: high block, equalizer, low block"><rect class="eq" x="210" y="140" width="100" height="40"/><text x="260" y="165" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">DP cell</text><path class="th" d="M230 140 V60 M290 140 V60"/><path class="th" d="M230 100 H290"/><path class="eq" d="M220 60 L240 80 L240 60 L220 80 z" transform="rotate(90 230 70)"/><path class="eq" d="M280 60 L300 80 L300 60 L280 80 z" transform="rotate(90 290 70)"/><path class="eq" d="M250 90 L270 110 L270 90 L250 110 z"/><text x="200" y="60" text-anchor="end" class="sm">high block</text><text x="320" y="60" class="sm">low block</text><text x="260" y="128" text-anchor="middle" class="am sm">equalizer</text><text x="230" y="30" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">HP</text><text x="290" y="30" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">LP</text></svg>`,
    bite: `An operator valved out a DP flow transmitter by closing the low-side block first. The reading jumped to maximum, and the flow controller slammed its valve shut, tripping the downstream unit. Controller in manual first, and follow the manifold order your site teaches.`,
    head: { q: 'Before touching a DP cell manifold for a transmitter that feeds a controller, what should happen to the controller?', a: 'It should be put in manual (per procedure) so the reading swing does not move the valve.' } },
  { title: 'Blowing a wet leg',
    floor: `If you have a straw full of water with your finger on top, the water stays put. Let go at the wrong time and it all runs out. A wet leg is a pipe kept full of liquid on purpose, and losing that liquid changes the reading.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="wet-leg">wet leg</span> is an impulse line kept full of a known liquid so a level or DP cell reads correctly. If someone opens a vent or drain on it ("blows it down") to clear it, the leg can empty or refill with the wrong liquid. The reading shifts, sometimes a lot, and it can stay wrong until the leg is refilled correctly. Always tell the board before touching impulse lines, and have instrument support refill and verify.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>What happened to the wet leg</th><th>Level reading (typical)</th></tr><tr><td>Normal, full of fill liquid</td><td>Correct</td></tr><tr><td>Drained or blown empty</td><td>Reads high (low side got lighter)</td></tr><tr><td>Filled with heavier process liquid</td><td>Reads low</td></tr><tr><td>Fill liquid boiling off (hot service)</td><td>Slowly drifts high</td></tr></table></div><p class="fig-note">Direction depends on how the cell is piped. The point: touching the leg changes the reading.</p>`,
    bite: `On nights, a tech blew down a wet leg to "clear a plug." The level suddenly read 95%. The level controller opened the outlet wide and pumped the tank toward empty before anyone caught it. Blowing a leg is a planned job with the board informed and the loop in manual.`,
    head: { q: 'Someone drained a wet leg. Can you still trust that level reading?', a: 'No. Not until the leg is refilled correctly and the reading is verified.' } },
  { title: 'Manual mode and walking away',
    floor: `Cruise control holds your speed on hills. If you turn it off and take your foot off the gas, the car slows down on the next hill. Putting a controller in manual is turning off cruise control. You are now the controller.`,
    words: `In <span class="term" data-term="manual-mode">manual</span>, the controller output (<span class="term" data-term="op">OP</span>) stays wherever you set it. It no longer reacts to the process. If flow, level, or temperature changes, nothing corrects it except you. Putting a loop in manual to deal with a problem is normal. Walking away without a handoff is how tanks overflow and pumps run dry. If you put it in manual, stay with it, or hand it off out loud and put it on the log.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Who moves the valve</th><th>Your job</th></tr><tr><td>Auto</td><td>Controller, toward SP</td><td>Watch PV, SP, OP</td></tr><tr><td>Manual</td><td>You, by setting OP</td><td>Watch PV constantly. Nothing else will correct it.</td></tr><tr><td>Cascade (if used)</td><td>Another controller sets this SP</td><td>Know which controller is in charge</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A level controller was put in manual during a pump swap at 2300. The swap went fine, but the operator got pulled to an alarm on the other side of the unit. At 0100 the tank overflowed into the dike. The controller did exactly what it was told: nothing.`,
    head: { q: 'You put a loop in manual. What are your two choices before you leave the board?', a: 'Return it to auto, or hand it off out loud to someone who will watch it (and log it).' } },
  { title: 'Do not trust one gauge',
    floor: `If your bathroom scale says you lost 20 pounds overnight, you step off and on again, or try another scale. One strange reading is a question, not an answer.`,
    words: `Every gauge and transmitter can fail: plugged taps, frozen lines, drift, a stuck needle, a dead battery on a wireless unit. Before acting on one number, confirm with a second, independent indication: a local gauge vs the board <span class="term" data-term="pit">PIT</span>, a <span class="term" data-term="sight-glass">sight glass</span> vs the level transmitter, pump amps vs flow. When something protects people (LOTO, opening equipment), this is not optional. Report a bad instrument with what you saw, when, and what you compared it with.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>One reading says</th><th>Second check</th></tr><tr><td>Pressure zero on a filter</td><td>Vent open and quiet, drain stopped</td></tr><tr><td>Level 20%</td><td>Sight glass, second transmitter, tank gauge</td></tr><tr><td>Flow 0</td><td>Pump amps, discharge pressure, downstream level</td></tr><tr><td>Temperature normal</td><td>Flow is actually passing the thermowell</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A pressure gauge on a vessel read 0 psig and the manway was opened. The gauge had a closed root valve. The vessel still held pressure. Two kinds of evidence would have caught it.`,
    head: { q: 'A gauge reads zero. What makes that trustworthy enough to open equipment?', a: 'A second, independent proof like an open, quiet vent and stopped drain, plus following the procedure.' } }
];

STEPS.control = [
  { title: 'PV, SP, OP in plain words',
    floor: `You are driving and want to go 55. The speedometer shows 50, so you press the gas more. What you want is 55. What you see is 50. How far you press the pedal is what you do about it. That is a control loop.`,
    words: `Every controller shows three numbers. <span class="term" data-term="pv">PV</span> (process variable) is what the process is actually doing right now, the speedometer. <span class="term" data-term="sp">SP</span> (setpoint) is what you want, the 55. <span class="term" data-term="op">OP</span> (output) is what the controller sends to the valve or pump, usually 0 to 100%, the gas pedal. In auto, the controller keeps adjusting OP to push PV toward SP.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Number</th><th>Car version</th><th>FIC-101 version</th></tr><tr><td>PV</td><td>Speedometer: 50</td><td>Measured flow: 180 gpm</td></tr><tr><td>SP</td><td>Want 55</td><td>Want 200 gpm</td></tr><tr><td>OP</td><td>Gas pedal: 40%</td><td>Signal to FV-101: 62%</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A new board operator saw OP at 62% and wrote "flow 62" on the log. OP is valve signal, not flow. PV is flow. Mixing them up leads to bad turnover notes and bad decisions.`,
    head: { q: 'Which number tells you what the process is doing right now?', a: 'PV, the process variable.' } },
  { title: 'How FIC-101 moves FV-101',
    floor: `When you want a warmer shower, you turn the handle a bit, wait, and feel. If it is still cold, you turn more. A flow controller does that dance every second.`,
    words: `The <span class="term" data-term="orifice">orifice</span> meter measures flow and sends it to <span class="term" data-term="fic">FIC-101</span> as PV. FIC-101 compares PV with SP. If flow is low, it raises OP, and <span class="term" data-term="control-valve">FV-101</span> opens more. If flow is high, it lowers OP and FV-101 closes some. The controller settings (tuning) decide how hard and fast it reacts. If OP reaches 100% and flow is still low, the controller has run out of room. Something else is limiting flow, like a plugged filter, a weak pump, or a closed block valve.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 200" role="img" aria-label="Loop diagram: FT measures flow, FIC compares to SP, sends OP to FV"><rect class="eq" x="200" y="20" width="120" height="60" rx="8"/><text x="260" y="45" text-anchor="middle" class="lb">FIC-101</text><text x="260" y="65" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">compares PV to SP</text><path class="ln" d="M20 160 H500"/><circle class="bub" cx="120" cy="120" r="20"/><text x="120" y="125" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">FT</text><path class="sig" d="M120 100 V50 H200"/><text x="130" y="45" class="bt sm">PV (flow)</text><path class="sig" d="M320 50 H400 V125"/><text x="330" y="45" class="bt sm">OP (%)</text><path class="eq" d="M385 148 L415 172 L415 148 L385 172 z"/><path class="eq" d="M385 132 A15 15 0 0 1 415 132 z"/><text x="430" y="150" class="sm">FV-101</text><text x="260" y="195" text-anchor="middle" class="sm">SP entered by the operator on the board</text></svg>`,
    bite: `FIC-101 OP sat at 100% for an hour with flow below SP. Nobody noticed because no alarm came in. The filter was plugging. An OP pinned at 0% or 100% means the controller cannot do its job. Treat it like an alarm.`,
    head: { q: 'OP is 100% and PV is still below SP. What does that tell you?', a: 'The controller is out of room. Something else is limiting flow. Look in the field.' } },
  { title: 'Auto, manual, and who is in charge',
    floor: `Autopilot flies the plane in cruise, but pilots take over for landing or when something odd happens. Knowing who is flying right now is the most important thing.`,
    words: `In auto, the controller moves OP. In <span class="term" data-term="manual-mode">manual</span>, you set OP. Some loops are cascade, where one controller sets another's SP. For example, a level controller like <span class="term" data-term="lic">LIC-101</span> can set the flow SP on FIC-101. Temperature loops like <span class="term" data-term="tic">TIC</span> and pressure loops like <span class="term" data-term="pic">PIC</span> work the same way. Before you change anything, read the mode. Changing SP on a loop in manual does nothing to the valve. Changing OP in auto gets overwritten.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>If the loop is in…</th><th>Changing SP does</th><th>Changing OP does</th></tr><tr><td>Auto</td><td>Controller moves OP to chase the new SP</td><td>Usually not allowed / overwritten</td></tr><tr><td>Manual</td><td>Nothing to the valve right now</td><td>Moves the valve directly</td></tr><tr><td>Cascade</td><td>SP comes from another controller</td><td>Not allowed</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `An operator kept raising SP on a temperature loop and nothing happened. The loop had been left in manual by the last shift. He added 20 degrees of SP before noticing. When it went back to auto, it chased that huge SP hard. Read the mode first.`,
    head: { q: 'You raise SP on a loop in manual. What happens to the valve?', a: 'Nothing. In manual, only OP moves the valve.' } },
  { title: 'Slamming OP without a plan',
    floor: `Stomp the gas pedal on ice and the car spins. Small, steady moves keep you on the road. Process equipment reacts the same way to big, sudden moves.`,
    words: `A big manual OP jump (say 30% to 90%) can cause a pressure surge (water hammer), pull a tank or suction down fast, trip a pump on low suction, lift a <span class="term" data-term="relief-valve">relief valve</span>, or upset the next unit. A plan looks like this: know what the valve feeds and what could trip; make small steps (like 2 to 5%); wait for PV to answer; watch the partner numbers. If the process is slow (temperature, big tanks), wait longer between moves.`,
    pic: `<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 520 200" role="img" aria-label="Big OP step causes PV overshoot; small steps settle smoothly"><path class="th" d="M40 180 H500 M40 180 V20"/><path class="red" d="M40 140 H120 V60 H260"/><path class="red" d="M120 150 C150 30 180 30 200 80 S240 110 260 95" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="140" y="30" class="rt sm">big slam → overshoot</text><path class="grn" d="M280 140 H310 V130 H340 V120 H370 V110 H400 V100 H500"/><path class="grn" d="M280 150 C320 145 350 125 380 115 S450 102 500 100" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="300" y="80" class="gt sm">small steps → steady</text><text x="45" y="195" class="sm">solid = OP, dashed = PV</text></svg>`,
    bite: `Flow was low on FIC-101 and the operator slammed FV-101 from 40% to 100% in manual. The downstream receiver pressure spiked, the level jumped, and a pump downstream tripped on high level. Three alarms from one move. Small steps would have found the problem without the trip.`,
    head: { q: 'Name one thing a big OP slam can cause.', a: 'Water hammer, a pump trip, a relief lifting, a level swing, or a downstream upset.' } }
];

STEPS.design = [
  { title: 'Why that pump',
    floor: `You would not tow a trailer with a sports car, even though both are cars. Engineers pick pumps the same way: the right tool for this liquid, this pressure, and this flow.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="centrifugal-pump">centrifugal pump</span> was likely picked for steady, thin liquids at good flow. A <span class="term" data-term="pd-pump">PD pump</span> was likely picked for thick liquids, slurry, exact metering, or high pressure at low flow. Pumps are sized for a design flow and head, with a best operating range. Running far below it (throttled way back) or far above it (wide open, low suction) shortens its life. That is <span class="term" data-term="design-intent">design intent</span>. For gas handling, engineers use compressors or blowers instead, and slurry pumps have hardened, open impellers.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>If you see…</th><th>Engineering probably wanted…</th></tr><tr><td>Centrifugal with a min-flow line</td><td>Protect it when downstream demand is low</td></tr><tr><td>PD pump with a relief back to suction</td><td>Protect against a blocked discharge</td></tr><tr><td>Two pumps, one spare</td><td>Keep running when one needs service</td></tr><tr><td>Slurry pump with flush water</td><td>Keep solids out of the seal</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A pump was always run with its discharge valve barely cracked because "that's how we get the right flow." It was oversized for today's rate. Seals failed every few months. The real fix was an engineering change, not a new seal every quarter.`,
    head: { q: 'What is "design intent" in plain words?', a: 'What the engineers built the equipment to do, and the conditions they expected it to run in.' } },
  { title: 'Why that valve',
    floor: `A kitchen faucet and a fire hydrant both control water, but you would not swap them. Valve choice depends on the job, the fluid, and what happens when things go wrong.`,
    words: `A <span class="term" data-term="globe-valve">globe valve</span> or characterized control valve was picked to throttle smoothly. A <span class="term" data-term="ball-valve">ball</span> or <span class="term" data-term="gate-valve">gate</span> was picked to isolate. A <span class="term" data-term="butterfly-valve">butterfly</span> was picked for big lines at low pressure where size and cost matter. The <span class="term" data-term="fail-position">fail position</span> was picked from a question: "If air is lost, which way is safe?" Materials were picked for the chemical, the temperature, and any solids. Swapping a valve type "because we had one in the shop" can change all of that.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Why (typical)</th></tr><tr><td>FV-101 is a globe style, fail close</td><td>Smooth throttling. Losing air stops feed to T-102.</td></tr><tr><td>Ball valves at pump suction</td><td>Tight isolation, low restriction</td></tr><tr><td>Check valve on pump discharge</td><td>Stop backflow when pump stops</td></tr><tr><td>Butterfly on cooling water</td><td>Big line, low pressure, lower cost</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A failed control valve was replaced with a spare that fit the flange, but it was fail open, not fail close. Nobody checked. When instrument air dropped months later, the tank overfilled. "It fits" is not the same as "it is the same."`,
    head: { q: 'A spare control valve fits the flange. What must you still confirm?', a: 'That it matches the design: fail position, trim/size, materials. That is a change that needs review.' } },
  { title: 'Why that filter',
    floor: `You would not use a coffee filter on a pool, or a pool filter for coffee. Filters are sized for how much dirt, how fine, and how much flow.`,
    words: `Engineers pick a <span class="term" data-term="strainer">strainer</span> to protect a pump from big debris, a <span class="term" data-term="cartridge-filter">cartridge</span> for fine polishing, a <span class="term" data-term="bag-filter">bag</span> for moderate dirt loads, and larger process filters with <span class="term" data-term="precoat">precoat</span> when solids are high or the solids are the product. They pick duplex housings where the process cannot stop for a change-out. Element micron rating, housing size, and DP limits are part of the design. Putting a finer element in "to get cleaner product" can plug it fast and starve a pump.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>If you see…</th><th>Design intent</th></tr><tr><td>Duplex strainer</td><td>Switch sides without stopping flow</td></tr><tr><td>DP gauge or PDT across the filter</td><td>So operators can see loading</td></tr><tr><td>Bypass with a locked or car-sealed valve</td><td>Only for controlled, approved use</td></tr><tr><td>Relief on the filter housing</td><td>Housing cannot take full pump pressure blocked in</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `Someone swapped in finer cartridges thinking "cleaner is better." DP hit the limit in two hours instead of two days, and the crew changed filters all night. The micron rating was part of the design for a reason.`,
    head: { q: 'Is a finer filter element always better?', a: 'No. It can plug fast, raise DP, and starve the pump. The rating is a design choice.' } },
  { title: 'Design intent vs how we actually run',
    floor: `Your house was built for four people. Add three more and a home business, and the plumbing and power start to complain. Plants drift the same way: more rate, new products, old equipment.`,
    words: `Over years, plants run differently from how they were designed: higher rates, different feeds, bypasses left open, valves always wide open, alarms that everyone ignores. Each of those is a sign the design and the operation have split apart. That is not always wrong, but it needs review. Sites use <span class="term" data-term="moc">MOC</span> (management of change) to review changes to equipment, chemicals, settings, and procedures before they become the new normal. The operator's job is to notice the drift and speak up. Your observations are how engineering finds out.`,
    pic: `<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Drift sign</th><th>What to do</th></tr><tr><td>Control valve always at 95–100%</td><td>Report. The loop has no room left.</td></tr><tr><td>Filter bypass cracked "all the time"</td><td>Report and log it. This needs review.</td></tr><tr><td>Same alarm every shift, everyone acknowledges</td><td>Report. It is either real or needs fixing.</td></tr><tr><td>Pump always throttled way back</td><td>Report. Possible sizing issue.</td></tr></table></div>`,
    bite: `A unit ran for a year with a filter bypass cracked open because "the filter can't keep up." When an audit found it, nobody knew who opened it or why. A heat exchanger downstream was badly fouled. One report a year earlier would have started the right fix.`,
    head: { q: 'Your control valve has been at 100% for weeks. What should you do?', a: 'Report it. The design and the operation have split, and it may need an engineering review (MOC).' } }
];

/* ---------- QUESTIONS (adaptive Check banks) ----------
   difficulty: 1 easy, 2 medium, 3 harder. answer = index into choices (choices are shuffled on screen).
   lessonStep is 1-based. Distractors are written to be plausible and similar in length to the right answer. */
const QUESTIONS = {};

QUESTIONS.pressure = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'A pressure gauge open to the air reads 0 psig. What does that zero mean?', choices: ['The gauge needs to be recalibrated', 'It matches the atmosphere around it', 'The line behind it is empty and safe to open', 'There is no air pushing on the gauge at all'], answer: 1, explain: 'Gauges read zero at atmosphere. The air is still pushing about 14.7 psi, but the gauge calls that zero.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Vacuum means…', choices: ['Pressure lower than the atmosphere around it', 'Any pressure that is measured in inches', 'A force inside the vessel that pulls on things', 'Pressure a little higher than the atmosphere'], answer: 0, explain: 'Vacuum is just less than atmosphere. The outside air does the pushing. Nothing "sucks."', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'What does the "g" in psig stand for?', choices: ['Gas, because it is used for gas lines', 'Gallons, for the flow through the line', 'Gauge: pressure above atmosphere', 'Gravity, the weight of the liquid'], answer: 2, explain: 'psig is gauge pressure: measured above the atmosphere. psia is absolute.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'A gauge reads 30 psig. About what is that in psia?', choices: ['About 60 psia', 'About 30 psia', 'About 15.3 psia', 'About 44.7 psia'], answer: 3, explain: 'psia ≈ psig + 14.7. So 30 + 14.7 ≈ 44.7 psia.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Vacuum on a vessel went from 22 in Hg to 15 in Hg. What happened to the pressure inside?', choices: ['It went down, deeper into vacuum', 'It went above atmosphere, into pressure', 'It went up, closer to atmosphere', 'It stayed the same, only units changed'], answer: 2, explain: 'Fewer inches of vacuum means less vacuum, so more gas is inside and pressure rose toward atmosphere.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'You are about to pump a closed storage tank empty. What do you do first to prevent tank collapse?', choices: ['Close the vent so vapor stays inside the tank', 'Confirm the vent or vacuum breaker path is open', 'Raise the pump speed so the job finishes faster', 'Watch the level transmitter and nothing else'], answer: 1, explain: 'Tanks collapse when liquid leaves and air cannot get in. Confirm the vent path is open and not blocked or frozen.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A pump suction normally reads +3 psig. Tonight it reads 10 in Hg vacuum and the discharge gauge bounces. Which move is WRONG for the first response?', choices: ['Check the tank level feeding the pump', 'Check the suction strainer for plugging', 'Open the discharge valve more for rate', 'Check the suction valve lineup and position'], answer: 2, explain: 'The problem is on the suction side. Opening the discharge pulls even harder on a starving suction and makes cavitation worse.', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'The log says "column pressure 15." Why is that note risky?', choices: ['It could mean 15 psia or 15 psig', 'Columns are never run at a pressure of 15', 'It is fine as long as the number is right', 'Pressure of 15 is always inches of water'], answer: 0, explain: '15 psia is about atmosphere. 15 psig is about 29.7 psia. Always write the unit with the letter.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A big thin-walled tank was steamed out, closed up, and left to cool overnight. What is the main danger?', choices: ['Steam condenses, leaving vacuum that can crush the tank', 'The level transmitter reads wrong once the tank cools off', 'Nothing, because a cool closed tank is always a safe tank', 'Cooling makes the inside pressure climb until the roof lifts'], answer: 0, explain: 'Cooling steam condenses to a small amount of water, leaving vacuum inside. With no vent path, outside air can crush the tank.', lessonStep: 4 }
];

QUESTIONS.dp = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'DP (differential pressure) is…', choices: ['The highest pressure anywhere in the system', 'Pressure measured in psia instead of psig', 'Upstream pressure minus downstream pressure', 'The pressure at the bottom of a full tank'], answer: 2, explain: 'DP is the difference between two pressures, like across a filter or an orifice.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Filter inlet 55 psig, outlet 47 psig. What is the filter DP?', choices: ['55 psi', '47 psi', '8 psi', '102 psi'], answer: 2, explain: '55 − 47 = 8 psi across the filter.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'As a filter collects dirt, its DP usually…', choices: ['Stays the same', 'Goes up', 'Goes down', 'Swings to zero'], answer: 1, explain: 'More dirt = more restriction = more push needed = higher DP.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'On an orifice flow meter, DP goes up. That usually means…', choices: ['A dirty filter', 'More flow', 'A full tank', 'Less flow'], answer: 1, explain: 'On an orifice meter, more flow makes more DP (about 4× DP for 2× flow).', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Suction 2 psig, discharge 48 psig, gauges at the same height, liquid full. About how much is the pump putting up?', choices: ['About 24 psi', 'About 50 psi', 'About 46 psi', 'About 96 psi'], answer: 2, explain: 'Discharge minus suction: 48 − 2 ≈ 46 psi.', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Filter DP suddenly drops from 18 psi to 1 psi and flow comes back up with no change-out. What is most likely?', choices: ['Normal behavior for a filter near its limit', 'The pump picked up speed and pushed harder', 'The element tore or a bypass opened', 'The dirt cake fell off and the filter cleaned itself'], answer: 2, explain: 'Filters do not clean themselves. A sudden DP drop often means dirt is going around or through a failed element.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A DP level cell was set up for water. The tank now holds a lighter liquid at the same height. The reading will be…', choices: ['Stuck at zero until recalibrated', 'Exactly right, height is height', 'Higher than the real level', 'Lower than the real level'], answer: 3, explain: 'Lighter liquid pushes less at the same height, so the cell sees less DP and reads low.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'An orifice flow meter slowly reads higher all night while production is flat. The low-side impulse line is leaking. Why does flow read high?', choices: ['Leaks always make flow meters read high', 'The orifice hole wore bigger overnight', 'Low side reads low, so DP looks bigger', 'The liquid got heavier as it cooled off'], answer: 2, explain: 'If the low side reads too low, high minus low looks bigger, and the meter "sees" more flow.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Suction 2 psig, discharge only 20 psig right after motor rewiring (normally 48). Flow is low but not zero. What should you suspect?', choices: ['The discharge gauge got bumped during the work', 'The suction tank is overfull and flooding it', 'The pump may be spinning backward', 'The pump is fine and just needs time to warm up'], answer: 2, explain: 'A centrifugal running backward still pumps a little, fooling people. Check rotation after electrical work.', lessonStep: 5 }
];

QUESTIONS.pid = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Which drawing shows every line, valve, and instrument?', choices: ['P&ID', 'Isometric', 'Plot plan', 'PFD'], answer: 0, explain: 'The P&ID (piping and instrumentation diagram) is the operator\'s detailed map.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Read this tag: FIC-101.', choices: ['Flow Indicating Controller', 'Flow Isolation Control valve', 'Fan Indicating Current meter', 'Filter Inlet Check valve'], answer: 0, explain: 'F = flow (what is measured). I = indicating, C = controller (what it does).', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'In an ISA-style tag, the FIRST letter tells you…', choices: ['What is being measured', 'The line size it is on', 'Which area it sits in', 'What the device does'], answer: 0, explain: 'First letter = measured thing (F, P, T, L, A, V). Following letters = what it does (I, R, C, T, A).', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'On a P&ID, a bow tie with a dome on top connected to a dashed signal line is a…', choices: ['Gate valve', 'Relief valve', 'Check valve', 'Control valve'], answer: 3, explain: 'The dome is the actuator. The signal line means a controller moves it.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Read this tag: PDT-101.', choices: ['Pressure Drain Tap valve', 'Pressure Differential Transmitter', 'Pump Discharge Temperature', 'Primary Density Transmitter'], answer: 1, explain: 'P = pressure, D = differential, T = transmitter.', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'A small trapezoid shape in a line on the P&ID means…', choices: ['A low-point drain', 'A reducer (size change)', 'A one-way check valve', 'A filter housing'], answer: 1, explain: 'A reducer looks like a small trapezoid and shows a size change.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'In the worked loop, flow on FIC-101 is low, FV-101 is 100% open, PDT-101 is high, and PIT-102 is up. What do you look at first?', choices: ['Tank T-102: check whether it is full', 'Filter F-101: it is loading up', 'FV-101: call instruments, it must be stuck', 'FIC-101: raise the setpoint to get more'], answer: 1, explain: 'High filter DP and higher pump discharge point at a restriction at the filter. FV-101 is wide open trying to make up for it.', lessonStep: 6 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'You plan an isolation from the PFD because it is simpler. What is the risk?', choices: ['PFDs use different tag numbers than the field', 'PFDs show so many valves it is easy to get lost', 'There is no risk as long as you are careful', 'It misses valves, bypasses, and drains'], answer: 3, explain: 'The PFD is big picture only. Use the current P&ID, and walk the line.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'The field has a bypass valve around FV-101 that is NOT on your P&ID. What do you do?', choices: ['Ignore it, because the drawing is always right', 'Sketch it on your copy and carry on', 'Close it, then keep going with the job', 'Stop and report the difference first'], answer: 3, explain: 'Drawings go out of date. Report field differences so the drawing gets fixed and nobody gets surprised.', lessonStep: 1 }
];

QUESTIONS.readings = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'A level of 0% on the board always means the tank is empty.', choices: ['True, 0% means nothing is left', 'True, unless the tank is outdoors', 'Only true on tanks with a closed roof', 'False, it is the bottom of the range'], answer: 3, explain: '0% is the bottom of the range. Liquid can still be below it.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'A temperature sensor usually sits inside a…', choices: ['Seal pot', 'Orifice plate', 'Thermowell', 'Strainer'], answer: 2, explain: 'A thermowell is a metal pocket that sticks into the process and holds the sensor.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Which pair of readings is best for checking a flow reading?', choices: ['Flow vs source and destination levels', 'Flow vs the time of day on the trend', 'Flow vs the outside air temperature', 'Flow vs the last shift\'s log entry'], answer: 0, explain: 'If flow is real, the source should fall and the destination should rise to match.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'A flow reading has been perfectly flat at 120 gpm for four hours on a live process. What do you suspect?', choices: ['The signal may be frozen or failed', 'The pump is dead-headed on recycle', 'The process is running perfectly', 'Nothing, flat lines are a good sign'], answer: 0, explain: 'Real process readings wiggle. Perfectly flat often means a frozen or failed signal.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Exchanger outlet temperature looks normal, but flow through it has dropped a lot. What do you do first?', choices: ['Ignore it, because the temperature is fine', 'Treat the low flow as real and go look', 'Raise the temperature setpoint a little', 'Put the loop in manual and move on'], answer: 1, explain: 'A good temperature with a bad flow is still trouble. Believe the bad number until proven otherwise.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Flow reads normal but the receiving tank level is not rising. What are the two possibilities?', choices: ['Tank is too big, or the pump is too small', 'Level transmitter drift, or a bad trend', 'Nothing is wrong, levels lag behind flow', 'Meter is wrong, or flow goes elsewhere'], answer: 3, explain: 'Either the meter lies or the product is going somewhere else (wrong line-up or leak). Check both.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Why can a temperature reading look fine while a heater is overheating?', choices: ['Heaters shut themselves off before they overheat', 'Thermowells read faster when flow is low', 'Temperature and flow have nothing to do with it', 'With little flow it reads still liquid'], answer: 3, explain: 'With no flow past the sensor, it does not see what is happening in the tubes.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Discharge pressure looks normal but pump amps are way low. Which is most likely?', choices: ['Pump dead-headed or losing prime', 'Suction flooded with extra liquid', 'Pump running at its best efficiency point', 'Motor overloaded and about to trip'], answer: 0, explain: 'Low amps on a centrifugal means low flow. Pressure can look fine against a closed or blocked path.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A tank shows 5%. You plan to transfer in a full batch. What should you know first?', choices: ['Real volume below 0% and above 100%', 'Nothing, since 5% is close to empty', 'Only the pump rate for the transfer', 'The outside temperature that night'], answer: 0, explain: 'Percent is of a set range. Know the real volume below 0% and above 100% before planning a transfer.', lessonStep: 2 }
];

QUESTIONS.pumps = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Which pump type must NEVER be started against a closed discharge?', choices: ['Neither, both are fine', 'Centrifugal', 'Any pump with a seal', 'Positive displacement'], answer: 3, explain: 'A PD pump moves a fixed amount per turn. Blocked discharge means pressure climbs until something gives.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'A pump sounds like it has gravel in it. That is usually…', choices: ['A loose coupling guard', 'Normal running noise', 'Too much suction pressure', 'Cavitation'], answer: 3, explain: 'Cavitation sounds like gravel or marbles: bubbles forming and collapsing in the pump.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: '"Priming" a centrifugal pump means…', choices: ['Greasing the bearings before start-up', 'Closing the suction valve before start', 'Filling the casing with liquid first', 'Running it fast for a minute to warm it up'], answer: 2, explain: 'Centrifugal pumps need liquid in the casing to pump. Air-bound pumps move almost nothing.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Should pump packing drip a little?', choices: ['Yes, a slow drip keeps it cool', 'No, packing should always be bone dry', 'Only when the pump is shut down', 'Only on hot days when it expands'], answer: 0, explain: 'Packing is designed to drip slowly. A dry, smoking gland means it is too tight.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'A pump is cavitating. What do you check FIRST?', choices: ['The discharge valve, and open it up more', 'The suction: level, valve, strainer, temperature', 'The seal flush flow and the seal pot level', 'The motor wiring and the breaker settings'], answer: 1, explain: 'Cavitation starts on the suction side when pressure at the inlet is too low.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Seal pot level on a pump is dropping steadily. What does it suggest?', choices: ['Barrier fluid is leaking out past the seal', 'Suction pressure is too high on the pump', 'The feed tank is overflowing into it', 'The pump is working exactly as designed'], answer: 0, explain: 'A falling seal pot level means barrier fluid is going somewhere, usually past the seal. Report it.', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A pump rattles only at night when the tank runs low and the product is warmer. What is the main issue?', choices: ['Vibration from other night-time equipment', 'The motor is too big for the pump', 'Low suction margin: low level, warm liquid', 'The discharge valve is open too far'], answer: 2, explain: 'Low level lowers suction pressure and warm liquid boils easier. Together they cause cavitation.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Amps dropped from 42 to 30 over two nights at the same valve position. Which is the best first response?', choices: ['Ignore it, lower amps just save power', 'Raise the motor overload trip setting', 'Report it and check flow and pressures', 'Pinch the suction valve to steady it'], answer: 2, explain: 'A slow change is still a change. Lower amps usually mean less flow: restriction, wear, or a coupling issue.', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'You start a centrifugal pump but the far-end valve is closed, and you get called away. What is the risk?', choices: ['The suction side will over-pressure', 'Dead-head: heat builds and the seal fails', 'The pump speeds up with no load on it', 'None, centrifugals are fine running closed'], answer: 1, explain: 'With no flow out, all the energy becomes heat. Never leave a dead-headed pump.', lessonStep: 2 }
];

QUESTIONS.valves = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Which valve is best for hand-throttling flow?', choices: ['Globe valve', 'Gate valve', 'Check valve', 'Plug valve'], answer: 0, explain: 'Globe valves are built to throttle smoothly. Gates are for fully open or fully closed.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'A check valve…', choices: ['Lets flow go one way only', 'Is used to throttle flow by hand', 'Measures the pressure in a line', 'Is closed by hand for isolation'], answer: 0, explain: 'Check valves are worked by the flow and stop backflow.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Instrument air is lost. A fail-close valve will…', choices: ['Go fully open', 'Swing back and forth', 'Go fully closed', 'Stay where it was'], answer: 2, explain: 'Fail close = the spring drives it shut when air or signal is lost.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Which valve is WRONG for throttling flow for months?', choices: ['Gate valve', 'Globe valve', 'Control valve', 'Throttling butterfly'], answer: 0, explain: 'Throttled gates chatter and get their seats cut, so later they will not isolate.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Cooling water to a condenser is usually set to fail…', choices: ['Open, so cooling stays on', 'Last, always, on every loop', 'Closed, to save water', 'It does not really matter'], answer: 0, explain: 'Losing cooling is dangerous, so cooling water valves are often fail open.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'The board says FV-101 output is 0%. Does that prove the valve is closed?', choices: ['Only when the loop is set to auto', 'Yes, as long as it is a fail-close valve', 'Yes, the board always matches the valve', 'No, check the local stem and the process'], answer: 3, explain: 'OP is what was asked for. The valve may be stuck, or the positioner may be off.', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Maintenance needs a line isolated to open a flange. Which is WRONG to count as isolation?', choices: ['Closed, locked block valves per procedure', 'The control valve sitting at 0% output', 'A blind installed per the procedure', 'Double block and bleed per procedure'], answer: 1, explain: 'Control valves pass and can move with the signal. They are not isolation.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A ball valve handle is across the pipe (looks closed), but pressure is building behind the flange. What is likely?', choices: ['The pressure gauge behind it is broken', 'Pressure always builds behind any valve', 'Handle on wrong or stem broken: it is open', 'Ball valves can never leak, so it is heat'], answer: 2, explain: 'Handles can be put on 90° off or the stem can shear. Verify with process proof.', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Two identical valves sit side by side and one tag is missing. What do you do?', choices: ['Open both a little and watch flow', 'Pick the one closest to the pump', 'Trace the pipe and check the P&ID', 'Leave it for the next shift to sort'], answer: 2, explain: 'Match the tag, the line, and the flow direction. Missing tags get replaced.', lessonStep: 1 }
];

QUESTIONS.vacuum = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Why do plants pull vacuum on evaporators and dryers?', choices: ['So liquids boil at lower temperature', 'To make the liquid heavier', 'To help the pumps run faster', 'To raise the pressure inside'], answer: 0, explain: 'Lower pressure lets liquids boil at lower temperatures, which is gentler on product.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Under vacuum, a leaking gasket leaks…', choices: ['Outward, so you see a drip', 'Not at all under vacuum', 'Inward, air gets sucked in', 'Only when the pump is off'], answer: 2, explain: 'Vacuum leaks go inward. Listen for a hiss.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'A steam ejector has…', choices: ['No moving parts', 'A ring of seal water', 'A piston and rod', 'A spinning impeller'], answer: 0, explain: 'Ejectors use fast motive steam through a nozzle to drag gas along. No moving parts.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Knockout pot level is high and vacuum is falling. What is likely?', choices: ['The receiver level is running too low', 'Liquid is carrying over into the pump', 'Vacuum is fine and the alarm is false', 'The condenser is running too cold'], answer: 1, explain: 'A full knockout pot lets liquid into the pump. Check the pot drain.', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Liquid-ring pump seal water is running hot. What happens?', choices: ['The pump reverses', 'Vacuum gets better', 'Nothing changes', 'Vacuum gets worse'], answer: 3, explain: 'Hot seal water flashes in the ring and the pump cannot pull as deep.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: '"We lost vacuum." What is the first check on the list?', choices: ['Open the vessel and look inside it', 'Raise the feed rate to load it up', 'Motive: steam or seal water, pump running', 'Break vacuum with air to reset it'], answer: 2, explain: 'Check the supplies first: motive steam or seal water, then cooling, knockout pot, discharge, leaks.', lessonStep: 6 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Vacuum is falling. The PIC-101 bleed valve is at 0% and still cannot reach setpoint. Steam, seal water, and cooling are normal. Most likely?', choices: ['The PIC is bleeding in too much gas', 'The condenser is running too cold', 'An air leak is loading the system', 'There is too little feed coming in'], answer: 2, explain: 'If the bleed is shut and supplies are normal, something else is letting gas in: a leak.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Vacuum dropped fast at shift change. Which response is WRONG?', choices: ['Check motive first, then cooling, then the pot', 'Raise steam, open the bleed, cut feed together', 'Make one change, then watch the result', 'Say each change on the radio and log it'], answer: 1, explain: 'Changing three things at once hides the real cause and can make things worse.', lessonStep: 6 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'An ejector\'s vacuum drifts down on a cold night. Steam pressure looks normal. What should you suspect?', choices: ['Nothing, cold nights improve vacuum', 'Too much cooling water on the condenser', 'The nozzle wore bigger in the cold', 'Wet motive steam from a failed trap'], answer: 3, explain: 'Ejectors need dry steam at design pressure. Wet steam hurts them even when the gauge looks normal.', lessonStep: 2 }
];

QUESTIONS.filtration = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'When a filter loads up with dirt, DP…', choices: ['Goes down', 'Goes up', 'Stays the same', 'Drops to zero'], answer: 1, explain: 'Filter DP up = more restriction.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'A strainer mainly catches…', choices: ['Dissolved chemicals in the liquid', 'Air bubbles before the pump', 'Big debris like rags and scale', 'Fine haze that makes product cloudy'], answer: 2, explain: 'Strainers are coarse screens that protect pumps and valves from big debris.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Before opening a filter housing, you must…', choices: ['Loosen all the lid bolts at the same time', 'Isolate, drain, vent, and verify zero', 'Read the gauge, and if it says zero, open it', 'Open it fast so less liquid spills out'], answer: 1, explain: 'Never open a filter under pressure. Verify zero more than one way.', lessonStep: 6 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'After a bag change, DP is much lower than any clean bag you have seen. What do you suspect?', choices: ['Flow is much higher than normal tonight', 'It is an extra-good bag from a new supplier', 'The pump is pushing harder than usual', 'The bag is not seated or is torn'], answer: 3, explain: 'Too-good DP after a change often means flow is going around the bag.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Filter DP is up, but flow was also raised 30%. Is the filter necessarily dirtier?', choices: ['Yes, a DP rise always means dirt', 'No, DP never changes with flow', 'Yes, flow has no effect on DP', 'No, more flow also raises DP'], answer: 3, explain: 'DP rises with flow as well as with dirt. Compare DP at similar rates.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'What is precoat?', choices: ['Paint on the inside of the filter housing', 'The first batch of dirty liquid through', 'A gasket coating that stops lid leaks', 'A filter-aid layer built before filtering'], answer: 3, explain: 'Precoat is a thin filter-aid layer that does the fine filtering.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Flow is low and filter DP is LOW too. Pump discharge pressure is low. What do you look at first?', choices: ['Swap the filter element to a new one', 'Open the filter bypass to get flow back', 'Upstream: pump, suction strainer, valves', 'Raise the flow setpoint on the controller'], answer: 2, explain: 'Low DP with low flow is not the filter. The problem is upstream.', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'DP is high and rate is suffering. Which move is WRONG without procedure approval?', choices: ['Plan a swap over to the clean side', 'Open the filter bypass to make rate', 'Tell the board and log what you see', 'Check flow and pump pressures first'], answer: 1, explain: 'The bypass sends unfiltered liquid downstream. Only with the procedure and the right approval.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A strainer gauge reads zero. The vent is closed. Can you open it?', choices: ['Yes, a zero reading means zero', 'Yes, as long as it feels cool', 'Yes, if the pump is switched off', 'No, vent it and prove zero first'], answer: 3, explain: 'Do not trust one gauge. Verify zero pressure more than one way.', lessonStep: 6 }
];

QUESTIONS.together = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'In the system story, what does FIC-101 move?', choices: ['P-101', 'KO-101', 'VP-101', 'FV-101'], answer: 3, explain: 'FIC-101 is the flow controller. It moves control valve FV-101.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'When several numbers move at once, the best habit is…', choices: ['Wait for day shift to come in and decide', 'Change everything quickly to stop it', 'Put every loop in manual and walk away', 'Find what moved first, change one thing'], answer: 3, explain: 'One change at a time lets you see what worked.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'As filter F-101 plugs, PDT-101…', choices: ['Stays the same', 'Reads vacuum', 'Rises', 'Falls'], answer: 2, explain: 'More restriction across the filter = more DP.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'FV-101 output creeps to 100% while PDT-101 climbs. What is the root cause?', choices: ['Tank T-101 is overfull', 'Vacuum pump VP-101 failing', 'FV-101 is broken and stuck', 'Filter F-101 is loading up'], answer: 3, explain: 'The valve is opening to make up for a plugging filter.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Feed flow into the vacuum receiver drops. Vacuum may…', choices: ['Always get worse right away', 'Deepen, since there is less vapor', 'Never change at all from feed', 'Go positive above atmosphere'], answer: 1, explain: 'Less feed means less flash vapor for the vacuum system to handle.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'On a centrifugal pump, as a downstream filter plugs and flow falls, amps usually…', choices: ['Drop to zero', 'Fall a bit', 'Stay exactly flat', 'Rise sharply'], answer: 1, explain: 'Less flow on a centrifugal usually means less power draw.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Trend: filter DP rose first, then flow fell, then vacuum changed. What do you address first?', choices: ['The condenser', 'The vacuum pump', 'The filter', 'The level controller'], answer: 2, explain: 'Address what moved first. The others followed.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Vacuum on VE-101 falls every afternoon when the feed tank warms in the sun. Most likely?', choices: ['The PIC loses its tuning when warm', 'Cooler feed is causing condensation', 'Hotter feed flashes more vapor', 'The vacuum pump wears out each afternoon'], answer: 2, explain: 'Warmer feed makes more vapor, which loads the vacuum system.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'During an upset, which is WRONG?', choices: ['Make one change and watch it', 'Call for help early on', 'Follow the upset procedure', 'Bypass an interlock for rate'], answer: 3, explain: 'Never defeat an interlock to make rate.', lessonStep: 4 }
];

QUESTIONS.loto = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'LOTO stands for…', choices: ['Lift off, turn over', 'Lockout/tagout', 'Low oil, top off', 'Lock on, test once'], answer: 1, explain: 'Lockout/tagout: controlling hazardous energy during work.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Can you remove another person\'s lock because they went home?', choices: ['Yes, if the job is urgent enough', 'Yes, at shift change it is expected', 'Yes, if you know them and call them', 'No, only by the formal removal process'], answer: 3, explain: 'Your lock protects you. Only a formal approved process can remove someone else\'s.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Which is NOT a form of hazardous energy?', choices: ['Heat in the casing', 'Pressure in a line', 'A compressed spring', 'A written procedure'], answer: 3, explain: 'Pressure, heat, chemical, electrical, gravity, and stored mechanical energy are all hazards. A procedure is not.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'In double block and bleed, the bleed between the blocks is…', choices: ['Open, to show and drain any leak-by', 'Cracked just slightly to throttle it', 'Closed, to hold pressure between the valves', 'Removed and capped for the whole job'], answer: 0, explain: 'The open bleed proves the first block is holding and keeps pressure off the second.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Why do a try-start after lockout?', choices: ['To prove the right source is off', 'To test that the motor still works', 'It is optional if labels are good', 'To warm the pump before the job'], answer: 0, explain: 'Labels and breakers can be wrong. The try-step catches it.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'A motor breaker is locked. What else could still hurt someone opening the pump?', choices: ['Header pressure through the discharge', 'Static from the paint on the casing', 'The motor nameplate voltage rating', 'Nothing, electrical is the only energy'], answer: 0, explain: 'Electrical isolation alone does not isolate liquid pressure, heat, or chemicals.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Which should NOT be counted as isolation?', choices: ['A blind installed per procedure', 'A locked closed block valve per procedure', 'A check valve in the line', 'Double block and bleed per procedure'], answer: 2, explain: 'Check valves can leak or stick. They are not isolation.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A control valve is isolated and its air is locked off. What energy may still be there?', choices: ['None, air was the only source', 'Only electrical in the positioner', 'A compressed actuator spring', 'Only heat left in the valve body'], answer: 2, explain: 'Actuator springs store mechanical energy. Know which parts hold it.', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Liquid is blocked in between two closed valves in the sun. What can happen?', choices: ['The liquid shrinks and pulls vacuum', 'Thermal expansion builds pressure', 'The valves ease open by themselves', 'Nothing, closed valves hold it safe'], answer: 1, explain: 'Heated trapped liquid expands and can build high pressure.', lessonStep: 5 }
];

QUESTIONS.failures = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'A "passing" valve is one that…', choices: ['Passed its last inspection', 'Leaks through while closed', 'Opens faster than normal', 'Has a missing valve tag'], answer: 1, explain: 'Passing means it does not seal when closed.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'A rupture disk, after it bursts…', choices: ['Stays open until replaced', 'Recloses when pressure drops', 'Can be reset by hand in place', 'Becomes stronger than before'], answer: 0, explain: 'Rupture disks are one-time devices.', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'A plugged suction strainer usually makes suction pressure…', choices: ['Stay the same', 'Go above discharge', 'Rise', 'Drop'], answer: 3, explain: 'The pump is starved, so suction pressure falls, sometimes into vacuum.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Controller output is 0% but flow is still high. What do you suspect?', choices: ['The pump has stopped running', 'The feed tank has run empty', 'Valve stuck open, or a bypass open', 'Everything is normal for that loop'], answer: 2, explain: 'The valve is not doing what it is told. Check the stem and the bypass in the field.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'After a job, vacuum will not come back to normal. Where do you look first?', choices: ['Whatever the job opened or touched', 'The vacuum pump motor nameplate', 'The control room trend screens', 'The far end of the unit first'], answer: 0, explain: 'Start with the last thing opened: gaskets, fittings, drains.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'A blocked-in tank level keeps creeping up overnight. What do you check?', choices: ['Open the outlet so the level holds', 'A passing inlet valve, and a 2nd reading', 'Log it as transmitter drift, move on', 'Nothing, tanks creep a bit at night'], answer: 1, explain: 'Creeping level in a blocked-in tank often means a passing valve. Confirm it is real.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A relief valve on a pump discharge has lifted twice tonight. Which response is WRONG?', choices: ['Report both lifts to the supervisor', 'Find out why the pressure is rising', 'Look for a closed valve downstream', 'Close the block under the relief'], answer: 3, explain: 'Never block, gag, or adjust a relief to keep running. It is the last protection.', lessonStep: 5 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A spare pump started and flow barely changed. The stopped pump\'s shaft is spinning backward. Why?', choices: ['That is normal after a pump swap', 'The suction header is flooded full', 'Stopped pump\'s check is stuck open', 'The new pump is too strong for the line'], answer: 2, explain: 'A stuck-open check lets flow run backward through the idle pump.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Pump running, discharge pressure high, no flow, casing getting hot. Most likely?', choices: ['Cavitation on the suction side', 'Normal warm-up after a start', 'A plugged suction strainer', 'Check stuck closed, or dead-headed'], answer: 3, explain: 'High discharge with no flow looks like a dead-head. A stuck-closed check can do that.', lessonStep: 3 }
];

QUESTIONS.instruments = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'A DP cell manifold usually has high block, low block, and…', choices: ['A rupture disk', 'A check valve', 'A sample pump', 'An equalizing valve'], answer: 3, explain: 'The equalizer lets both sides see the same pressure.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'In manual, the controller output…', choices: ['Goes straight to zero', 'Stays where you set it', 'Follows the PV up and down', 'Keeps chasing the setpoint'], answer: 1, explain: 'In manual you are the controller. Nothing else corrects it.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Before acting on one strange reading, you should…', choices: ['Act on it right away', 'Confirm with a second reading', 'Recalibrate it yourself', 'Ignore it until it alarms'], answer: 1, explain: 'Do not trust one gauge.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Before touching a DP cell manifold that feeds a controller, the controller should be…', choices: ['Put in manual per procedure', 'Set to 100% output first', 'Left in auto so it can follow', 'Turned off at the breaker'], answer: 0, explain: 'The reading will swing. Manual keeps the valve from chasing it.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'A wet leg was drained. Can you trust that level reading?', choices: ['Only during the day shift', 'Yes, as long as it reads normal', 'No, not until refilled and checked', 'Yes, it is more accurate now'], answer: 2, explain: 'The leg sets the reference. Empty or wrong-filled legs shift the reading.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'You put a level loop in manual. Before leaving the board you must…', choices: ['Set the output to 100% first', 'Silence its alarms for an hour', 'Return to auto or hand off out loud', 'Nothing, manual holds it steady'], answer: 2, explain: 'Walking away from a manual loop is how tanks overflow.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Closing the low-side block first on a DP flow transmitter makes the reading…', choices: ['Swing, maybe to max, and slam the valve', 'Always drop to read exactly zero', 'Become more accurate than before', 'Stay perfectly steady throughout'], answer: 0, explain: 'Wrong order puts pressure on one side and swings the reading.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Which is WRONG to do to make rate?', choices: ['Call the instrument techs out', 'Compare with a second reading', 'Bypass an interlock unapproved', 'Report a suspect transmitter'], answer: 2, explain: 'Never defeat an interlock to make rate.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A vessel gauge reads 0 psig but its root valve is closed. What does the gauge tell you?', choices: ['Nothing about the vessel', 'The vessel has been drained', 'The vessel is under vacuum', 'The vessel is at zero pressure'], answer: 0, explain: 'A closed root valve cuts the gauge off from the process.', lessonStep: 4 }
];

QUESTIONS.control = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Which number shows what the process is doing right now?', choices: ['OP', 'Mode', 'SP', 'PV'], answer: 3, explain: 'PV = process variable, the measured value.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'SP means…', choices: ['Speed of the pump', 'Setpoint: what you want', 'Steam pressure supply', 'Signal percent to valve'], answer: 1, explain: 'SP is the target.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'OP is…', choices: ['The measured process value', 'The operator on the board', 'The outlet pressure reading', 'The output sent to the valve'], answer: 3, explain: 'OP is usually 0–100% signal to the final element.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'FIC-101 sees flow below setpoint. In auto, it will…', choices: ['Do nothing until alarmed', 'Raise OP to open FV-101', 'Switch itself to manual', 'Lower OP to close FV-101'], answer: 1, explain: 'Low flow → open the valve more.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'You raise SP on a loop that is in manual. The valve…', choices: ['Opens to chase the new SP', 'Swings open and shut', 'Closes a little bit', 'Does not move at all'], answer: 3, explain: 'In manual only OP moves the valve.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'OP is 100% and PV is still below SP. That means…', choices: ['The loop is working perfectly', 'The setpoint is set too low', 'It is out of room: look in the field', 'The PV transmitter is surely broken'], answer: 2, explain: 'Something else is limiting flow: filter, pump, block valve.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Flow is low. Which manual move is WRONG?', choices: ['Slam OP from 40% to 100%', 'Watch partner readings', 'Step OP 2–5% and watch', 'Check the field first'], answer: 0, explain: 'Big slams cause surges, trips, and upsets downstream.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A log says "flow 62" but 62 was the OP. Why is that a problem?', choices: ['62 is too high for that line', 'OP is valve signal, not flow', 'It is not a problem at all', 'OP and PV are the same thing'], answer: 1, explain: 'Mixing OP with PV leads to bad decisions.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A temperature loop was left in manual with a huge SP entered. What happens when it goes to auto?', choices: ['It shuts the unit down', 'It goes back to the old SP', 'Nothing happens at all', 'It chases the big SP hard'], answer: 3, explain: 'Read the mode and SP before switching. Big gaps cause big moves.', lessonStep: 3 }
];

QUESTIONS.design = [
  { difficulty: 1, q: '"Design intent" means…', choices: ['How we happen to run it today', 'The vendor name on the nameplate', 'The paint color and the tag style', 'What it was built to do, and for what'], answer: 3, explain: 'Design intent is the engineers\' plan for the equipment.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'MOC stands for…', choices: ['Motor operating current', 'Minimum operating capacity', 'Management of change', 'Main operating console'], answer: 2, explain: 'MOC reviews changes before they become the new normal.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 1, q: 'Which pump type is usually picked for thick liquids or exact metering?', choices: ['Centrifugal', 'Steam ejector', 'Positive displacement', 'Axial fan'], answer: 2, explain: 'PD pumps move a fixed amount each turn.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'A spare control valve fits the flange. What must you still confirm?', choices: ['Nothing, if it fits it is the same', 'Fail position, trim, and materials', 'Just that it weighs about the same', 'Only that the paint color matches'], answer: 1, explain: '"It fits" is not "it is the same." That is a change.', lessonStep: 2 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Is a finer filter element always better?', choices: ['No, it can plug fast and starve a pump', 'Only on the night shift runs', 'Yes, as long as it fits the housing', 'Yes, cleaner is always better'], answer: 0, explain: 'Micron rating is a design choice.', lessonStep: 3 },
  { difficulty: 2, q: 'Why is a relief valve often on a PD pump discharge?', choices: ['To prime the pump before start', 'To keep the motor running cool', 'To protect against a blocked discharge', 'To add extra flow when needed'], answer: 2, explain: 'PD pumps build pressure without limit when blocked.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'Your control valve has been at 100% for weeks. Best move?', choices: ['Report it: the loop has no room', 'Retune the loop yourself', 'Open the bypass for good', 'Ignore it while flow is okay'], answer: 0, explain: 'Report drift between design and operation.', lessonStep: 4 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A pump is always throttled way back and seals fail every few months. Likely cause?', choices: ['Seals from the wrong supplier color', 'Bad luck with a batch of seals', 'Oversized pump, far from its best range', 'Too much suction pressure on it'], answer: 2, explain: 'Running far from the design point shortens pump life. That needs an engineering fix.', lessonStep: 1 },
  { difficulty: 3, q: 'A filter bypass has been cracked open "for a while." What should happen?', choices: ['Pull the filter out entirely', 'Quietly close it and move on', 'Leave it, since it is working', 'Report and log it for review'], answer: 3, explain: 'A temporary workaround that becomes permanent needs review through your site change process.', lessonStep: 4 }
];

/* ---------- KB (Plant Mentor knowledge base) ----------
   answer: 3–6 sentences, bottom-up (feel it → plant version). simpler: one or two plain lines.
   supervisor: a coaching take. module/step: where to read more (step is 1-based). */
const KB = {};

Object.assign(KB, {
  'pressure': { title: 'Pressure', tags: ['pressure','psi','push','gauge','force'], module: 'pressure', step: 1,
    answer: 'Press your palm on a table: that push spread over your hand is pressure. In the plant, pressure is how hard a liquid or gas pushes on each square inch of pipe or vessel, measured in psi. Most field gauges read zero at atmosphere, so they show pressure above the air around you (psig). Pressure pushes things out of leaks, lifts relief valves, and is what makes an opened flange dangerous.',
    simpler: 'Pressure is push. More pressure, more push on every inch of the pipe.',
    supervisor: 'Coach the habit of always saying the unit (psig or psia) and never treating one gauge as proof of zero.' },
  'atmosphere': { title: 'Atmosphere (the baseline)', tags: ['atmosphere','air','baseline','14.7','barometric','ambient'], module: 'pressure', step: 1,
    answer: 'You are standing at the bottom of an ocean of air. It pushes on you at about 14.7 psi at sea level, but you are used to it so it feels like nothing. Plant gauges are built to call that push zero. Vacuum is anything below it, and gauge pressure is anything above it. The number shifts a little with weather and altitude.',
    simpler: 'The air around us pushes about 14.7 psi. Gauges call that zero.',
    supervisor: 'Ask new operators what "0 psig" really means. If they say "empty," teach the baseline.' },
  'psig': { title: 'psig (gauge pressure)', tags: ['psig','gauge','pressure','units'], module: 'pressure', step: 2,
    answer: 'Think of a scale you zero with a bag already on it: everything you read is extra above the bag. psig is pressure above the atmosphere, what most field gauges read. A tire at 32 psig is 32 psi above the air around it. To get absolute, add about 14.7: psia ≈ psig + 14.7.',
    simpler: 'psig = pressure above the air. Most gauges read psig.',
    supervisor: 'Insist logs and radio calls include the unit: "48 psig," not "48."' },
  'psia': { title: 'psia (absolute pressure)', tags: ['psia','absolute','pressure','units','vacuum'], module: 'pressure', step: 2,
    answer: 'Imagine measuring from a perfectly empty space with no air at all. That is the zero for psia. Atmosphere is about 14.7 psia, so psia ≈ psig + 14.7. Vacuum work and design numbers often use psia because it can never go below zero. A vessel at 5 psia is about 9.7 psi below atmosphere.',
    simpler: 'psia counts from perfect empty. The air around us is about 14.7 psia.',
    supervisor: 'When a crew mixes up psig and psia, stop and write both on the board so the unit sticks.' },
  'vacuum': { title: 'Vacuum', tags: ['vacuum','vac','suction','inches','hg','negative','below','atmosphere'], module: 'vacuum', step: 1,
    answer: 'Start with the atmosphere: the air around you is always pushing at about 14.7 psi. Vacuum is simply any pressure lower than that. When you suck on a straw you remove some air, and the outside air pushes the drink up. Nothing inside is "pulling." In the plant, vacuum lets liquids boil at lower temperature, helps drying and transfers, and is measured in inches of mercury (in Hg), inches of water (in WC), or absolute units. More inches of vacuum means less gas inside.',
    simpler: 'Vacuum = less air than outside. The outside air does the pushing.',
    supervisor: 'Listen for people saying vacuum "went up." Coach them to say "deeper" or "weaker" so nobody gets confused.' },
  'inches-hg': { title: 'Inches of mercury (in Hg)', tags: ['inches','hg','mercury','vacuum','units'], module: 'pressure', step: 3,
    answer: 'Old barometers used a glass tube of mercury: the air pushed it up about 30 inches. That is where "inches of mercury" comes from. On a vacuum gauge, in Hg tells you how far below atmosphere you are. About 29.9 in Hg is a perfect vacuum at sea level. 1 psi is about 2 in Hg.',
    simpler: 'in Hg is a vacuum unit. 0 = atmosphere, about 30 = perfect vacuum.',
    supervisor: 'Have crews convert one reading out loud each shift: "10 in Hg vac is about 5 psi below air."' },
  'inches-wc': { title: 'Inches of water column (in WC)', tags: ['inches','water','column','wc','draft','small pressure'], module: 'pressure', step: 3,
    answer: 'Blow gently into a U-shaped tube of water and the water moves a few inches. That tiny push is measured in inches of water column. It is used for very small pressures: tank vents, furnace draft, blower pressure. About 27.7 in WC equals 1 psi, so a few inches WC is a very small pressure. But a few inches WC of vacuum over a big tank roof can still crush it.',
    simpler: 'in WC measures tiny pressures. 27.7 in WC is only 1 psi.',
    supervisor: 'Make sure people know tank vents work in inches WC, so "just a little vacuum" can still collapse a tank.' },
  'dp': { title: 'DP (differential pressure)', tags: ['dp','differential','delta','pressure','difference','filter','across'], module: 'dp', step: 1,
    answer: 'Put your thumb over a hose end: pressure builds behind your thumb and is lower in front. That difference is DP. In the plant, DP is upstream pressure minus downstream pressure, across a filter, a valve, an orifice meter, or a column of liquid. On a filter, DP up means more restriction. On an orifice flow meter, DP up usually means more flow. A DP cell or PDT measures it.',
    simpler: 'DP = the pressure on one side minus the other side.',
    supervisor: 'Ask: "If this DP is real, what else should have changed?" Train people to pair DP with flow and pump pressures.' },
  'dp-cell': { title: 'DP cell', tags: ['dp','cell','transmitter','level','flow','manifold'], module: 'dp', step: 4,
    answer: 'Picture a see-saw with pressure pushing on each end. A DP cell is a transmitter with a high side and a low side that reads the difference. It is used for flow (across an orifice), level (weight of liquid), and filter DP. Its impulse lines can plug, freeze, or fill with the wrong liquid, and that makes it read wrong. Manifold valves must be worked in the right order.',
    simpler: 'A DP cell reads the difference between two pressures.',
    supervisor: 'Make manifold work a named, trained task. Wrong order has tripped units.' },
  'pdt': { title: 'PDT (pressure differential transmitter)', tags: ['pdt','differential','transmitter','filter','dp'], module: 'dp', step: 1,
    answer: 'Two gauges on a filter, one before and one after. Subtract them in your head and you have DP. A PDT does that for you and sends it to the board. PDT-101 in our loop watches the filter F-101. Rising PDT means the filter is loading, a sudden drop can mean a torn element or open bypass, and a flat line can mean the instrument itself is stuck.',
    simpler: 'PDT sends the filter DP to the board.',
    supervisor: 'Ask crews to know clean and change-out PDT values for each filter by heart.' },
  'pit': { title: 'PIT (pressure indicating transmitter)', tags: ['pit','pressure','transmitter','indicator','suction','discharge'], module: 'pid', step: 5,
    answer: 'A regular gauge shows pressure where you stand. A PIT shows it locally and also sends it to the board. In our loop, PIT-101 is pump suction pressure and PIT-102 is pump discharge. Discharge minus suction roughly tells you what the pump is putting up. If a PIT and a local gauge on the same line disagree, trust neither until you know why.',
    simpler: 'PIT = pressure gauge that also talks to the board.',
    supervisor: 'Have operators compare a board PIT with a local gauge on rounds, and report mismatches.' },
  'orifice': { title: 'Orifice plate (flow by DP)', tags: ['orifice','plate','flow','meter','dp','ft'], module: 'dp', step: 3,
    answer: 'Blow through a straw gently, then hard: the harder you blow, the more pressure builds in your mouth. An orifice plate is a plate with a hole clamped in a pipe. More flow makes more DP across it, and faster than flow does: double the flow is about four times the DP. The transmitter turns DP into a flow number. It assumes a certain density and a full pipe, so bubbles, a density change, or a plugged impulse line make it read wrong.',
    simpler: 'Orifice: more flow, more DP. The transmitter turns DP into flow.',
    supervisor: 'Coach: if a flow number disagrees with tank levels, suspect the meter before chasing the process.' },
  'wet-leg': { title: 'Wet leg and dry leg', tags: ['wet','leg','dry','impulse','level','dp','reference'], module: 'dp', step: 4,
    answer: 'Keep a straw full of water with your finger on top and the water stays put. A wet leg is an impulse line kept full of a known liquid on purpose so a level cell has a steady reference. A dry leg is kept empty of liquid. If a wet leg drains, boils off, or fills with the wrong liquid, the level reading shifts. Blowing down a wet leg is a planned job with the board told and the loop in manual.',
    simpler: 'A wet leg is a pipe kept full on purpose so a level reads right.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if anyone blows a wet leg without telling the board.' },
  'pump-head': { title: 'Pump developed pressure (head)', tags: ['head','pump','discharge','suction','developed','psi'], module: 'dp', step: 5,
    answer: 'Carrying a bucket up stairs adds energy to it. A pump adds energy to liquid. With gauges at about the same height and the lines full of liquid, discharge minus suction is roughly what the pump is putting up. Example: suction 2 psig, discharge 48 psig, so the pump is putting up about 46 psi. If that shrinks at the same flow, suspect wear, cavitation, air, or reverse rotation.',
    simpler: 'Discharge minus suction ≈ what the pump adds.',
    supervisor: 'Have crews log both pump pressures, not just discharge.' }
});

Object.assign(KB, {
  'pid': { title: 'P&ID (piping and instrumentation diagram)', tags: ['pid','p&id','drawing','diagram','piping','instrumentation','map'], module: 'pid', step: 1,
    answer: 'Think of a street map that shows every stop sign and turn. The P&ID is the plant\'s detailed map: every line, valve, instrument, and control loop, with tags. It shows how things connect, not where they physically sit. Use the current revision for line-ups and isolation support, and walk the line, because drawings can be out of date.',
    simpler: 'P&ID = the detailed map of pipes, valves, and instruments.',
    supervisor: 'Ask operators to trace one flow path on the P&ID and name each valve before a line-up.' },
  'pfd': { title: 'PFD (process flow diagram)', tags: ['pfd','process','flow','diagram','overview'], module: 'pid', step: 1,
    answer: 'Directions that say "take the highway to town" skip the side streets. A PFD is that big-picture view: main equipment, main flows, and often design rates. It does not show every valve, drain, or bypass. It is great for understanding what a unit does, but not for isolation.',
    simpler: 'PFD = the big picture. Not enough detail for isolation.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if anyone plans an isolation from a PFD.' },
  'isometric': { title: 'Isometric drawing ("iso")', tags: ['isometric','iso','piping','3d','routing'], module: 'pid', step: 1,
    answer: 'Picture a 3-D sketch of how a garden hose snakes around your yard. An isometric shows real pipe routing, elevations, lengths, and fittings for one line, mostly for pipe fitters and maintenance. It helps you find a line in a crowded pipe rack. It does not show control loops the way a P&ID does.',
    simpler: 'Iso = a 3-D-looking drawing of how one pipe really runs.',
    supervisor: 'Pair isos with P&IDs when training people to find lines in the field.' },
  'isa-tag': { title: 'ISA-style instrument tags', tags: ['tag','isa','letters','instrument','naming','read','fic','pic','tic'], module: 'pid', step: 5,
    answer: 'License plates follow a pattern, and so do instrument tags. The first letter is what is measured: F flow, P pressure, T temperature, L level, A analysis, V vibration. The next letters say what the device does: I indicate, R record, C control, T transmit, A alarm, V valve. So FIC-101 is a flow indicating controller, and FV-101 is the valve it moves. The number ties the loop together.',
    simpler: 'First letter = what is measured. Next letters = what it does.',
    supervisor: 'Quiz tags at turnover. Misread tags send people to the wrong instrument.' },
  'reducer': { title: 'Reducer', tags: ['reducer','pipe','size','fitting','trapezoid'], module: 'pid', step: 2,
    answer: 'A funnel goes from wide to narrow. A reducer is a pipe fitting that changes pipe size, drawn as a small trapezoid on the P&ID. Liquid speeds up when a pipe gets smaller. Reducers at pump suctions are often installed flat on top so air pockets do not collect.',
    simpler: 'Reducer = where a pipe changes size.',
    supervisor: 'Use reducers as a quick "can you read the drawing" check for new people.' },
  'tank': { title: 'Tank / vessel', tags: ['tank','vessel','storage','drum','receiver','t-101'], module: 'pid', step: 3,
    answer: 'A tank is a big bucket, but it has to breathe. As liquid goes in, air or vapor must get out through a vent, and as liquid goes out, air must get in. Blocked vents can over-pressure or collapse a tank. On the P&ID a tank or vessel is a tall rounded rectangle with a tag like T-101 or VE-101. Know what 0% and 100% level mean on each one.',
    simpler: 'A tank is a big container that needs its vent open to breathe.',
    supervisor: 'Make vent checks part of every pump-out or cool-down.' },
  'heat-exchanger': { title: 'Heat exchanger', tags: ['heat','exchanger','cooler','heater','shell','tube','e-101'], module: 'pid', step: 3,
    answer: 'Hold a cold drink against a warm hand: heat moves from hot to cold through the can wall. A heat exchanger moves heat between two fluids without mixing them, like cooling water taking heat from a product. Dirt (fouling) and air blanketing make it work worse. A condenser is a heat exchanger that turns vapor back into liquid.',
    simpler: 'An exchanger moves heat from one fluid to another through a wall.',
    supervisor: 'Coach pairing: outlet temperature with both flows.' },
  'filter': { title: 'Filter', tags: ['filter','filtration','element','housing','f-101'], module: 'filtration', step: 1,
    answer: 'A coffee filter holds back grounds and lets coffee through. A plant filter holds dirt, protects what is downstream (pump seals, nozzles, exchanger tubes, product), or recovers solids when solids are the product. Its DP tells you how loaded it is. Never open one under pressure: isolate, drain, vent, and verify zero.',
    simpler: 'A filter catches dirt. DP tells you how full it is.',
    supervisor: 'Ask what job each filter does. That tells you what a bypass costs.' },
  'centrifugal-pump': { title: 'Centrifugal pump', tags: ['centrifugal','pump','impeller','p-101'], module: 'pumps', step: 1,
    answer: 'A box fan pushes air by spinning. A centrifugal pump spins an impeller and flings liquid outward. It needs to be primed (full of liquid). If the discharge is closed, pressure tops out but the pump heats up. Flow drops as discharge pressure rises. Gravel noise usually means cavitation on the suction side.',
    simpler: 'A spinning pump that flings liquid. It must be full of liquid to work.',
    supervisor: 'Have operators explain the pre-start checks for a centrifugal out loud.' },
  'pd-pump': { title: 'Positive displacement (PD) pump', tags: ['positive','displacement','pd','gear','piston','diaphragm','screw','lobe','pump'], module: 'pumps', step: 1,
    answer: 'A bicycle pump pushes a fixed amount of air each stroke. A PD pump (gear, lobe, screw, piston, diaphragm) moves a fixed amount per turn. Block its discharge and pressure keeps climbing until a relief lifts or something breaks. Never start one against a closed valve. Control flow with speed or a recycle, not by throttling shut.',
    simpler: 'PD pumps push a fixed amount every turn. Never block the discharge.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if a PD pump is started with a closed discharge.' },
  'prime': { title: 'Prime', tags: ['prime','priming','air','bound','casing','fill'], module: 'pumps', step: 2,
    answer: 'A straw only works if the bottom is in the drink. A centrifugal pump only pumps if its casing is full of liquid: that is prime. If it fills with air or vapor it becomes "air-bound" and moves almost nothing, and it can heat up. Priming usually means filling and venting the casing per procedure before start.',
    simpler: 'Prime = the pump is full of liquid, not air.',
    supervisor: 'Ask how they know a pump is primed before start. "It usually is" is not an answer.' },
  'dead-head': { title: 'Dead-head', tags: ['dead','head','deadhead','closed','discharge','no flow','blocked'], module: 'pumps', step: 2,
    answer: 'Stir a cup of coffee for ten minutes and it gets warmer. A dead-headed pump keeps stirring the same liquid with no flow out, so the energy turns into heat. The liquid can boil and wreck the seal and impeller. On a PD pump it is worse: pressure climbs until something gives. Minimum flow lines exist to prevent this.',
    simpler: 'Dead-head = pump running with nowhere for the liquid to go.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if a pump is left running against a closed discharge.' },
  'min-flow': { title: 'Minimum flow line', tags: ['minimum','flow','recycle','min','protection'], module: 'pumps', step: 2,
    answer: 'Think of leaving a faucet dripping so pipes do not freeze. A minimum flow line sends a little pump discharge back to the tank so the pump always has some flow, even when downstream wants none. It keeps the pump from heating up when dead-headed. Do not close it unless the procedure says so.',
    simpler: 'A small return line that keeps a pump from running with zero flow.',
    supervisor: 'Ask why the min-flow line exists. Closed min-flow valves kill pumps.' },
  'cavitation': { title: 'Cavitation', tags: ['cavitation','cavitate','gravel','marbles','noise','suction','bubbles','npsh'], module: 'pumps', step: 3,
    answer: 'Think of a garden hose: put your thumb over the end and the water speeds up and sprays. Now picture the pump\'s suction side, where pressure drops as liquid rushes into the impeller eye. If it drops low enough, the liquid boils into tiny bubbles even at normal temperature, and they collapse hard on the high-pressure side. That sounds like gravel in the pump. In the plant, look at the suction side first: tank level, suction valve, strainer, and liquid temperature. It eats impellers and seals.',
    simpler: 'Cavitation = the liquid boils inside the pump because suction pressure is too low. It sounds like gravel.',
    supervisor: 'Coach: gravel noise means check suction first, never "open the discharge more."' },
  'npsh': { title: 'NPSH (net positive suction head)', tags: ['npsh','suction','head','margin','cavitation','boil'], module: 'pumps', step: 3,
    answer: 'Picture how hard you can suck on a straw before the drink starts to fizz. NPSH is the plain question: is there enough pressure at the pump inlet, above the liquid\'s boiling point at that temperature, to keep it liquid? Low tank level, hot liquid, plugged strainers, and too much flow all eat that margin. Run out of margin and the pump cavitates.',
    simpler: 'NPSH = enough suction pressure to keep the liquid from boiling in the pump.',
    supervisor: 'Ask what eats NPSH margin on your pumps: level, temperature, strainer, rate.' },
  'vapor-pressure': { title: 'Vapor pressure', tags: ['vapor','pressure','boil','boiling','flash'], module: 'vacuum', step: 1,
    answer: 'Pasta water boils at a lower temperature on a mountain because there is less air pushing down. Every liquid has a vapor pressure: the pressure at which it boils at a given temperature. Warmer liquid has higher vapor pressure, so it boils more easily. Drop the pressure below it (vacuum, pump suction) and it flashes to vapor. This is why vacuum lets you boil gently and why warm liquid cavitates pumps.',
    simpler: 'Vapor pressure = the pressure where a liquid starts to boil.',
    supervisor: 'Connect this to both vacuum and cavitation so the idea sticks.' },
  'seal': { title: 'Mechanical seal', tags: ['seal','mechanical','seal pot','flush','leak','barrier'], module: 'pumps', step: 4,
    answer: 'A faucet stem has a small seal so water does not dribble out around it. A mechanical seal does that on a spinning pump shaft using two very flat faces, one turning and one still. It should not visibly leak. Many have a flush or seal pot with barrier fluid. Watch the seal pot level and pressure: rising level means process leaking in, falling means barrier leaking out.',
    simpler: 'A seal keeps liquid from leaking along the spinning shaft. It should stay dry.',
    supervisor: 'Make seal pot level a logged round item, not just a glance.' },
  'packing': { title: 'Packing', tags: ['packing','gland','drip','rope','shaft'], module: 'pumps', step: 4,
    answer: 'Picture rope rings squeezed around a shaft. That is packing. Unlike a mechanical seal, packing should drip slowly (a few drops a minute) to stay cool and lubricated. No drip plus a hot or smoking gland means it is too tight. A steady stream means it needs adjustment or replacement by someone trained.',
    simpler: 'Packing is rope rings around the shaft. A slow drip is healthy.',
    supervisor: 'Coach: "no leak" on packing is not always good news.' },
  'amps': { title: 'Motor amps', tags: ['amps','current','motor','load','power'], module: 'pumps', step: 5,
    answer: 'A vacuum cleaner motor sounds different when the hose is blocked. Motor amps tell you how hard the motor is working. On a centrifugal pump, more flow usually means more amps and less flow means fewer amps. A big change at the same valve position means something changed: restriction, wear, loss of prime, or a coupling problem.',
    simpler: 'Amps = how hard the motor is working.',
    supervisor: 'Ask for amps on every pump round and compare with last shift.' },
  'gate-valve': { title: 'Gate valve', tags: ['gate','valve','isolation','block','throttle'], module: 'valves', step: 4,
    answer: 'A gate valve is like a garage door: a flat gate slides across the pipe. Fully open it barely restricts flow, and fully closed it seals. It is for isolation, not throttling. Used half-open, it chatters and fast flow cuts the seats, so later it will not seal when you need it.',
    simpler: 'Gate valves are all-open or all-closed valves.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if a gate valve is being used to throttle.' },
  'globe-valve': { title: 'Globe valve', tags: ['globe','valve','throttle','control'], module: 'valves', step: 2,
    answer: 'A kitchen faucet handle lets you set a trickle or a full stream. A globe valve does that for plant piping. A plug moves toward a seat to throttle flow smoothly. Most control valves are globe style. It has more pressure drop than a gate when fully open, which is the price of good control.',
    simpler: 'Globe valves are made for throttling.',
    supervisor: 'Ask which valve on a line is meant for throttling. It is usually the globe or the control valve.' },
  'ball-valve': { title: 'Ball valve', tags: ['ball','valve','quarter','turn','isolation'], module: 'valves', step: 2,
    answer: 'Picture a ball with a hole through it: turn it a quarter turn and the hole lines up or blocks the pipe. Ball valves are quick, tight isolation valves. The handle usually lines up with the pipe when open and across it when closed, but a handle can be put on wrong, so verify with process proof.',
    simpler: 'Quarter-turn valve: handle in line = open, across = closed (usually).',
    supervisor: 'Coach checking stem flats or indicators, not just the handle.' },
  'butterfly-valve': { title: 'Butterfly valve', tags: ['butterfly','valve','disk','large','cooling water'], module: 'valves', step: 2,
    answer: 'A damper in an air duct is a disk that turns on a shaft. A butterfly valve is that disk in a pipe. It is compact and cheap for large lines like cooling water. Some are made to throttle and some are for on/off. Check the indicator or gearbox pointer, and remember pointers can slip.',
    simpler: 'A turning disk in the pipe. Common on big lines.',
    supervisor: 'Ask whether a given butterfly is meant to throttle or isolate.' },
  'check-valve': { title: 'Check valve', tags: ['check','valve','backflow','one way','non-return'], module: 'valves', step: 2,
    answer: 'A one-way door that swings open when you push from one side and shuts if pushed from the other: that is a check valve. Flow opens it, and backflow closes it. It stops liquid running backward when a pump stops. It can stick open (pump spins backward) or stick closed (no flow, like a dead-head). It is not an isolation valve.',
    simpler: 'Check valves let flow go one way only.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if a check valve is being counted as isolation.' },
  'control-valve': { title: 'Control valve (FV)', tags: ['control','valve','fv','actuator','positioner','throttle','fv-101'], module: 'valves', step: 3,
    answer: 'Cruise control moves your gas pedal for you. A control valve is a throttling valve with an actuator that a controller moves automatically, like FIC-101 moving FV-101. Its output on the board (OP) is what was asked for, not proof it moved. Check the local stem pointer. Every control valve has a fail position. It is not isolation.',
    simpler: 'A valve that a controller moves for you.',
    supervisor: 'Coach: know each control valve\'s fail position and where its block and bypass valves are.' },
  'fail-position': { title: 'Fail position (fail open / close / last)', tags: ['fail','open','close','last','fo','fc','fl','air','position'], module: 'valves', step: 3,
    answer: 'A screen door with a spring closes by itself when you let go. A control valve\'s spring drives it to a chosen fail position when air or signal is lost. Fail close (FC) is used where stopping flow is safe, like feed into a tank. Fail open (FO) is used where flow is safe, like cooling water. Fail last (FL) holds position. The drawing often marks FC, FO, or FL.',
    simpler: 'Where the valve goes when it loses air: open, closed, or stays put.',
    supervisor: 'Ask: "Instrument air just dropped. Which valves on your unit shut?"' },
  'throttle': { title: 'Throttling', tags: ['throttle','throttling','partly','open','control flow'], module: 'valves', step: 2,
    answer: 'Turning a faucet half-way to set a gentle flow is throttling. In the plant, throttling means holding a valve part-open to control flow, a job for globe valves and control valves. Throttling with gate valves wears their seats. Throttling a PD pump discharge shut is dangerous.',
    simpler: 'Throttle = hold a valve part-open to control flow.',
    supervisor: 'Coach "right valve for the job": throttle with globes, isolate with gates and balls.' },
  'isolation': { title: 'Isolation', tags: ['isolation','isolate','block','separate','energy'], module: 'loto', step: 2,
    answer: 'Closing the valve under a sink before fixing the faucet is isolation. In the plant, isolation separates equipment from every energy source: liquid, gas, steam, electricity, heat. Types range from a single block valve, to double block and bleed, to a blind. Your site procedure says which is required. Control valves and check valves are not isolation.',
    simpler: 'Isolation = cutting equipment off from everything that could hurt you.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if the isolation used is weaker than the procedure requires.' },
  'bleed': { title: 'Double block and bleed', tags: ['double','block','bleed','dbb','vent','isolation'], module: 'loto', step: 2,
    answer: 'Two closed doors with an open window between them: anything that sneaks past the first door goes out the window instead of reaching you. Double block and bleed means two closed block valves with an open bleed between them. The bleed shows whether the first valve is passing and keeps pressure off the second. It is a common isolation for pressure work.',
    simpler: 'Two closed valves with an open drain between them.',
    supervisor: 'Ask what they would do if the bleed keeps flowing. Correct answer: stop and report.' },
  'blind': { title: 'Blind', tags: ['blind','spade','blank','positive','isolation'], module: 'loto', step: 2,
    answer: 'Screwing a cap onto a pipe end is the surest way to stop it leaking. A blind is a solid plate put between flanges to positively block a line. It is stronger isolation than valves because it does not depend on a seat sealing. Installing and removing blinds is controlled by procedure and often a blind list.',
    simpler: 'A solid plate that blocks a pipe completely.',
    supervisor: 'Make sure blind lists are walked and checked before and after the job.' },
  'loto': { title: 'LOTO (lockout/tagout)', tags: ['loto','lockout','tagout','lock','tag','energy','permit'], module: 'loto', step: 1,
    answer: 'Before changing a light fixture, you flip the breaker and test the wire. LOTO is the plant version: isolate every energy source, lock and tag it, then verify zero energy before work. Energy includes electrical, pressure, heat, chemical, gravity, and stored mechanical. Only the person who applied a lock removes it, except through the formal process. This app is support training only. Your site procedure and permits come first.',
    simpler: 'Lock it, tag it, prove it is dead. Follow your site procedure.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if zero energy has not been verified or a lock or tag is missing.' },
  'zero-energy': { title: 'Verify zero energy', tags: ['zero','energy','verify','try','test','prove'], module: 'loto', step: 4,
    answer: 'After you flip the breaker, you test the wire before touching it. Verify zero energy means proving every source is off: try-start and confirm nothing moves, open vents and confirm they are quiet, confirm drains stopped, check temperature, and confirm moving parts have stopped. Do not trust one gauge. If anything is not at zero, stop and find out why.',
    simpler: 'Prove it is off. Do not just believe it is off.',
    supervisor: 'Watch for skipped try-steps. They are the step that catches wrong breakers.' }
});

Object.assign(KB, {
  'ejector': { title: 'Steam ejector', tags: ['ejector','steam','jet','motive','eductor','vacuum'], module: 'vacuum', step: 2,
    answer: 'Blow hard across the top of a straw standing in water and the water climbs. A steam ejector shoots high-pressure motive steam through a nozzle so it drags gas out of the vacuum system with it. It has no moving parts. It needs dry steam at design pressure, cooling water on its condenser, and low back pressure. Wet steam or low steam pressure make vacuum weaker.',
    simpler: 'Fast steam drags gas out and makes vacuum. No moving parts.',
    supervisor: 'Ask where motive steam pressure is read and what "normal" is.' },
  'liquid-ring': { title: 'Liquid-ring vacuum pump', tags: ['liquid','ring','vacuum','pump','seal water','vp-101'], module: 'vacuum', step: 2,
    answer: 'Spin a bucket of water and the water climbs the walls, making a ring. A liquid-ring pump spins an off-center impeller in a ring of seal water. The pockets between the blades grow and shrink, pulling gas in and squeezing it out. It needs cool seal water at steady flow. Hot seal water or low seal flow weakens vacuum, and liquid carryover from a full knockout pot can overload it.',
    simpler: 'A pump that uses a spinning ring of water to pull vacuum.',
    supervisor: 'Make seal water flow and temperature logged round items.' },
  'dry-vacuum-pump': { title: 'Dry vacuum pump', tags: ['dry','vacuum','pump','screw','lobe'], module: 'vacuum', step: 2,
    answer: 'Picture two tight-fitting screws that trap and carry gas along. A dry vacuum pump pulls vacuum without seal liquid. It hates liquid slugs and solids because the clearances are tiny. Knockout pots, condensers, and purges protect it.',
    simpler: 'A vacuum pump with no seal water. Keep liquid out of it.',
    supervisor: 'Ask how the dry pump is protected from liquid on your unit.' },
  'condenser': { title: 'Condenser', tags: ['condenser','cooling','water','vapor','condense','e-101'], module: 'vacuum', step: 5,
    answer: 'Breathe on a cold window and your breath turns to water drops. A condenser uses cooling water to turn vapor back into liquid. On a vacuum system that shrinks the gas load a lot, so the vacuum pump or ejector only has to handle what does not condense, like air. Lose cooling water or blanket the tubes with air, and vacuum falls.',
    simpler: 'A condenser turns vapor back into liquid with cooling water.',
    supervisor: 'Coach: cooling water is check #2 on lost vacuum.' },
  'knockout-pot': { title: 'Knockout pot', tags: ['knockout','ko','pot','separator','carryover','liquid','ko-101'], module: 'vacuum', step: 5,
    answer: 'A range hood grease trap catches drips before they reach the fan. A knockout pot catches liquid drops out of a gas stream before they reach a vacuum pump, compressor, or flare. If its level gets high or its drain plugs, liquid carries over and floods the machine. Check its level and drain on every round, and after any job.',
    simpler: 'A pot that catches liquid before it gets into the pump.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if a knockout pot drain is found closed with no explanation.' },
  'seal-water': { title: 'Seal water', tags: ['seal','water','liquid ring','flow','temperature'], module: 'vacuum', step: 5,
    answer: 'The water ring in a liquid-ring pump is what does the work. Seal water keeps that ring cool and full. If it gets hot it flashes, and if flow drops the ring gets thin. Either way vacuum gets worse. Watch flow, temperature, and the separator it returns to.',
    simpler: 'Seal water must be cool and steady for the pump to pull vacuum.',
    supervisor: 'Ask what the normal seal water temperature and flow are.' },
  'air-leak': { title: 'Air leak on vacuum', tags: ['air','leak','vacuum','hiss','gasket','inleak'], module: 'vacuum', step: 4,
    answer: 'Suck on a straw with a pin hole in it: you pull harder and get less. Under vacuum, leaks go inward, so you will hear a hiss rather than see a drip. Air fills the vacuum device, blankets the condenser, and makes vacuum fall. A PIC bleed at 0% that still cannot reach setpoint is a classic clue. Check whatever was touched last: gaskets, sight glasses, fittings, and sample valves.',
    simpler: 'Air sneaking into a vacuum system. Listen for a hiss.',
    supervisor: 'Ask: "What was opened on this system in the last day?"' },
  'lost-vacuum': { title: 'We lost vacuum: first five checks', tags: ['lost','vacuum','losing','falling','first','checks','dropping','weak'], module: 'vacuum', step: 6,
    answer: 'When your car will not start, you check fuel and battery before pulling the engine. For lost vacuum, go in order and change one thing at a time. First the motive (steam to the ejector, or seal water to the liquid-ring pump, and is the pump running), then condenser cooling water, then the knockout pot level and drain. Next the discharge path (vent, separator, back pressure), and last leaks and load: recent work, open valves, hissing, or a big change in process vapor. Your site upset procedure comes first.',
    simpler: 'Check the supplies (steam or seal water), then cooling, then the pot, then the outlet, then leaks.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if the crew is changing several things at once. Have one person call out each change on the radio.' },
  'strainer': { title: 'Strainer', tags: ['strainer','basket','y-strainer','screen','debris','suction'], module: 'filtration', step: 2,
    answer: 'A sink basket catches food chunks before they go down the drain. A strainer is a coarse screen (Y-type or basket) that catches rags, scale, and weld slag to protect pumps and valves. A plugged suction strainer starves the pump: suction pressure drops, amps drop, and it may cavitate. Expect them to plug fast after new work or tank cleaning.',
    simpler: 'A strainer is a coarse screen that protects the pump.',
    supervisor: 'Make strainer DP (or suction pressure) part of pump rounds.' },
  'cartridge-filter': { title: 'Cartridge filter', tags: ['cartridge','filter','element','micron','fine'], module: 'filtration', step: 2,
    answer: 'A water pitcher cartridge catches fine particles. Plant cartridge filters use replaceable tubes rated by particle size (micron). Change them at the DP limit. Finer is not always better, because a finer cartridge plugs faster and can starve a pump. Check O-rings and seating on every change.',
    simpler: 'Replaceable tubes that catch fine dirt.',
    supervisor: 'Ask what micron rating is specified and why.' },
  'bag-filter': { title: 'Bag filter', tags: ['bag','filter','sock','basket'], module: 'filtration', step: 2,
    answer: 'A vacuum cleaner bag holds dirt in a fabric pouch. A bag filter is a fabric bag in a basket for moderate dirt loads. If the bag ring is not seated, liquid goes around it, and DP looks great while doing nothing. A DP that is "too good" after a change means go check the seal.',
    simpler: 'A fabric bag that catches dirt. It must be seated right.',
    supervisor: 'Have a second person check bag seating on critical services.' },
  'filter-dp': { title: 'Filter DP climbing', tags: ['filter','dp','climbing','rising','high','plugging','loading','change'], module: 'filtration', step: 3,
    answer: 'A vacuum cleaner bag gets harder to pull air through as it fills. Filter DP climbing means the filter is getting more restricted, as long as flow has not gone up, because more flow also raises DP. Confirm it is real: flow down and pump discharge up? Before swapping, check for a pinched valve or a wrong duplex line-up, and remember low DP with low flow points upstream at the pump or suction strainer. Do not open the bypass unless the procedure allows it, and never open a housing under pressure.',
    simpler: 'Filter DP going up = the filter is getting clogged (if flow did not go up).',
    supervisor: 'Ask: "Show me the clean baseline and the change-out limit for this filter."' },
  'precoat': { title: 'Precoat (filter aid)', tags: ['precoat','filter','aid','diatomaceous','cake'], module: 'filtration', step: 4,
    answer: 'A sand bed in a fish tank filter does the fine filtering, and the screen just holds the sand. Precoat is a thin layer of filter aid powder built on a filter screen or cloth before the real process liquid goes through. The layer does the fine filtering. Patchy precoat lets dirt through.',
    simpler: 'A powder layer put on the filter first to do the fine filtering.',
    supervisor: 'Ask how they know the precoat is even before switching to process.' },
  'body-feed': { title: 'Body feed', tags: ['body','feed','filter','aid','cake'], module: 'filtration', step: 4,
    answer: 'Mixing a little sand into muddy water keeps the mud from sealing the filter. Body feed means adding filter aid into the dirty liquid while running, so the cake stays open. If body feed stops, filter DP climbs much faster than normal.',
    simpler: 'Filter aid added while running to keep the filter from sealing up.',
    supervisor: 'When DP climbs fast, ask: "Is body feed running?"' },
  'bypass': { title: 'Bypass', tags: ['bypass','around','temporary','filter','valve'], module: 'filtration', step: 3,
    answer: 'A detour around a road closure gets you there, but skips whatever the closure was for. A bypass is a line around a filter, control valve, or other equipment. Opening it can make numbers look better right away, but it sends unfiltered or uncontrolled flow downstream. Only use it if the procedure allows, with the right approval, and log and tag it.',
    simpler: 'A detour line around equipment. Use it only by procedure.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if a bypass is open with no log entry or approval.' }
});

Object.assign(KB, {
  'fic': { title: 'Reading FIC-101', tags: ['fic','fic-101','flow','controller','read','loop','how'], module: 'control', step: 2,
    answer: 'Think of cruise control: you set a speed, the speedometer shows actual speed, and the pedal moves to close the gap. FIC-101 is a Flow Indicating Controller. Read three numbers: PV is the measured flow right now, SP is the flow you want, and OP is the percent signal to FV-101. Check the mode too: auto, manual, or cascade. If OP is pinned at 100% and PV is still below SP, the controller is out of room, so look in the field (filter, pump, block valve).',
    simpler: 'FIC-101: PV = flow now, SP = flow wanted, OP = how open it is asking FV-101 to be.',
    supervisor: 'Ask operators to read PV, SP, OP, and mode out loud at turnover.' },
  'pic': { title: 'PIC (pressure indicating controller)', tags: ['pic','pic-101','pressure','controller','vacuum'], module: 'vacuum', step: 3,
    answer: 'A thermostat holds room temperature. A PIC holds pressure. PIC-101 on the vacuum receiver may hold vacuum by bleeding in a little gas or throttling the suction to the vacuum pump. If its output is pinned wide open or shut and it still misses setpoint, the problem is somewhere else, like a leak, motive, or cooling.',
    simpler: 'A PIC is a controller that holds pressure (or vacuum).',
    supervisor: 'Ask what PIC-101 actually moves to control vacuum on your unit.' },
  'lic': { title: 'LIC (level indicating controller)', tags: ['lic','level','controller','lic-101'], module: 'readings', step: 2,
    answer: 'Picture keeping a bathtub at the same level by opening and closing the drain. An LIC holds tank level by moving a valve, usually on the outlet or the inlet. Level readings are percent of a range, not feet. If the level transmitter lies, the LIC will faithfully do the wrong thing, so confirm with a sight glass or second level.',
    simpler: 'An LIC holds a tank level by moving a valve.',
    supervisor: 'Ask which valve the LIC moves and what happens if its transmitter freezes.' },
  'tic': { title: 'TIC (temperature indicating controller)', tags: ['tic','temperature','controller','tic-101'], module: 'readings', step: 3,
    answer: 'A home thermostat turns the furnace on and off to hold temperature. A TIC holds a process temperature, often by moving steam or cooling water. Temperature changes slowly, so give it time after every change. It needs flow past the sensor to mean anything.',
    simpler: 'A TIC holds a temperature by moving steam or cooling.',
    supervisor: 'Coach patience: temperature loops need time to answer.' },
  'pv': { title: 'PV (process variable)', tags: ['pv','process','variable','measured','actual'], module: 'control', step: 1,
    answer: 'Your speedometer shows how fast you are really going. PV is what the process is actually doing right now, the measured flow, pressure, level, or temperature. It comes from one instrument, so if the instrument lies, the PV lies.',
    simpler: 'PV = what is really happening right now.',
    supervisor: 'Ask: "How would you prove this PV is real?"' },
  'sp': { title: 'SP (setpoint)', tags: ['sp','setpoint','target','want'], module: 'control', step: 1,
    answer: 'The speed you set on cruise control is the setpoint. SP is the value you want the controller to hold. In auto, the controller moves OP to push PV toward SP. In manual, changing SP does nothing to the valve.',
    simpler: 'SP = the number you want.',
    supervisor: 'Coach checking mode before changing SP.' },
  'op': { title: 'OP (controller output)', tags: ['op','output','percent','valve','signal','mv'], module: 'control', step: 1,
    answer: 'How far you press the gas pedal is the output. OP is the signal the controller sends to the valve or pump, usually 0 to 100%. It is what was asked for, not proof the valve moved. OP pinned at 0% or 100% means the controller is out of room.',
    simpler: 'OP = how hard the controller is pushing the valve.',
    supervisor: 'Correct anyone who logs OP as if it were flow.' },
  'manual-mode': { title: 'Manual mode', tags: ['manual','mode','auto','hand','walk away'], module: 'instruments', step: 3,
    answer: 'Turn off cruise control and the car only does what your foot does. In manual, the controller output stays where you set it and nothing corrects the process except you. Manual is normal for troubleshooting, but walking away from a manual loop without a handoff is how tanks overflow and pumps run dry.',
    simpler: 'Manual = you are the controller now. Do not walk away.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if loops are left in manual with no handoff.' },
  'manifold': { title: 'Instrument manifold', tags: ['manifold','3-valve','5-valve','equalize','block','dp'], module: 'instruments', step: 1,
    answer: 'Picture a see-saw: if one kid jumps off suddenly, the other slams down. A DP transmitter manifold has a high block, a low block, and an equalizer (5-valve adds vents). Working them in the wrong order puts full pressure on one side, which can damage the cell and swing the reading. That can move a controller or trip an interlock. Put the loop in manual and follow your site\'s order.',
    simpler: 'A small valve block on a DP transmitter. Order matters.',
    supervisor: 'Only trained, authorized people work manifolds. Ask who that is on your shift.' },
  'interlock': { title: 'Interlock', tags: ['interlock','trip','shutdown','permissive','bypass','safety'], module: 'instruments', step: 1,
    answer: 'A microwave will not run with the door open. That is an interlock. Plant interlocks stop pumps, close valves, or block starts when conditions are unsafe. Never defeat, jumper, or bypass one to make rate. Bypassing needs your site\'s approval process, a time limit, and extra watch.',
    simpler: 'An automatic safety that stops things when conditions are unsafe.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if an interlock is bypassed without the required approval.' },
  'relief-valve': { title: 'Relief valve (and vacuum breaker)', tags: ['relief','valve','psv','prv','lift','safety','vacuum breaker','vent'], module: 'failures', step: 5,
    answer: 'A pressure cooker has a weighted valve that jiggles to let steam out and then settles back. A relief valve is spring-loaded and lifts when pressure reaches its set point, then recloses, though it may leak afterward. A vacuum breaker is its cousin: it lets air in so a tank is not crushed. If a relief lifts, pressure got too high. Find why and report it. Never block, gag, or adjust one to keep running.',
    simpler: 'A relief valve opens by itself to let out pressure that got too high.',
    supervisor: 'Treat every relief lift as a reportable event, and ask what caused it.' },
  'rupture-disk': { title: 'Rupture disk', tags: ['rupture','disk','burst','disc','relief'], module: 'failures', step: 5,
    answer: 'A pressure cooker\'s backup plug pops out for good when things get really bad. A rupture disk is a thin metal disk that bursts at a set pressure and stays open until replaced. It is often used alone or under a relief valve. A burst disk means the system saw a serious pressure. Get the area safe, report it, and follow the procedure.',
    simpler: 'A one-time metal disk that bursts to let out dangerous pressure.',
    supervisor: 'Make sure burst indicators and alarms are known and checked.' },
  'passing-valve': { title: 'Passing valve', tags: ['passing','leak','through','by','seat','valve'], module: 'failures', step: 2,
    answer: 'An old faucet that still drips when turned off hard is passing. A passing valve leaks through while closed, from cut seats, debris, or not being fully shut. Clues: a blocked-in tank level creeping, a bleed that keeps flowing, or pressure coming back after venting. This is why double block and bleed and verify steps exist.',
    simpler: 'A valve that leaks through even when closed.',
    supervisor: 'Encourage reporting passing valves, even small ones. They get worse.' },
  'design-intent': { title: 'Design intent', tags: ['design','intent','engineering','why','capacity'], module: 'design', step: 1,
    answer: 'A house built for four people strains when seven move in. Design intent is what the engineers built the equipment to do and the conditions they expected: fluid, flow, pressure, and temperature. Running far outside it, like always wide open, always bypassed, or a different fluid, shortens equipment life and can create hazards. Noticing and reporting that drift is part of the operator job.',
    simpler: 'Design intent = what it was built to do.',
    supervisor: 'Ask: "Designed for what, and how are we running it today?"' },
  'moc': { title: 'MOC (management of change)', tags: ['moc','management','change','review','permanent'], module: 'design', step: 4,
    answer: 'Before you knock out a wall at home, you check if it holds up the roof. MOC is how sites review changes to equipment, chemicals, settings, and procedures before they happen, so hidden risks get caught. A "temporary" workaround that stays is a change too. Use your site\'s process. This app does not replace it.',
    simpler: 'MOC = review a change before you make it.',
    supervisor: 'Stop the job if a workaround has become permanent without review.' }
});

Object.assign(KB, {
  'dcs': { title: 'DCS (the board)', tags: ['dcs','board','console','control','system','screen'], module: 'readings', step: 1,
    answer: 'Think of a car dashboard that also lets you steer. The DCS (distributed control system) is the computer system behind the board: it shows readings, runs controllers, sounds alarms, and keeps trends. Every number on it comes from a field instrument that can fail, so trends and paired readings matter more than any one number.',
    simpler: 'The DCS is the computer system you run the unit from.',
    supervisor: 'Teach trend reading early. It is the best troubleshooting tool on the board.' },
  'flow-reading': { title: 'Flow reading on the board', tags: ['flow','reading','gpm','fi','meter','rate'], module: 'readings', step: 1,
    answer: 'Watching a bucket fill tells you flow. A board flow reading comes from one meter in one spot. It can be fooled by bubbles, a partly empty pipe, density changes, or a plugged tap, and a failed signal can freeze at its last value. Confirm with tank levels, pump amps, and valve position.',
    simpler: 'Flow = how much is moving. Check it against tank levels.',
    supervisor: 'Ask: "What would prove this flow is real?"' },
  'level-reading': { title: 'Level reading on the board', tags: ['level','reading','percent','tank','range','lt'], module: 'readings', step: 2,
    answer: 'A coffee maker window shows the water line. Board level is usually percent of a set range, so 0% may not be empty and 100% may not be full. Foam, density changes, plugged taps, and wet leg problems fool it. Confirm with a sight glass or second level before big moves.',
    simpler: 'Level % is of a range, not of the whole tank.',
    supervisor: 'Make sure every operator knows what 0% and 100% mean on each tank.' },
  'temp-reading': { title: 'Temperature reading on the board', tags: ['temperature','reading','ti','hot','cold'], module: 'readings', step: 3,
    answer: 'A meat thermometer reads the spot at its tip. A board temperature reads one spot in a thermowell, and lags a little because heat has to soak through metal. With no flow past it, it reads still liquid or the pipe, not the process. Pair temperature with flow.',
    simpler: 'Temperature reads one spot, and only means something if fluid is moving past.',
    supervisor: 'Coach the flow + temperature pairing.' },
  'thermowell': { title: 'Thermowell', tags: ['thermowell','well','temperature','sensor','pocket'], module: 'readings', step: 3,
    answer: 'A metal pocket that sticks into the pipe, like a straw capped at the bottom. The temperature sensor sits inside it, so it can be removed without opening the process. The metal adds a small lag. A thermowell is a pressure boundary, so never pull one out of a live line.',
    simpler: 'A metal pocket in the pipe that holds the temperature sensor.',
    supervisor: 'Make sure people know a thermowell holds back process pressure.' },
  'sight-glass': { title: 'Sight glass / level glass', tags: ['sight','glass','gauge','level','window'], module: 'readings', step: 2,
    answer: 'The water window on a coffee maker lets you see the level. A sight glass does the same on a tank or pot, and it is the classic second check for a level transmitter. Its valves can be closed or its glass stained, so make sure it is lined up and clear. Under vacuum, a bad sight glass gasket can be an air leak.',
    simpler: 'A window that shows the real liquid level.',
    supervisor: 'Have operators compare glass vs transmitter on rounds.' }
});
